window.harnessChunks["rows-f13eedbbd792d17e.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-28T01:59:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kkkarthik6/duo-agents-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Duo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-28\">2026-11-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kkkarthik6/duo-agents-harness/tree/ab366d9ed0dfd327b9ed536e234ff2ce219351a4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ab366d9ed0dfd327b9ed536e234ff2ce219351a4\">ab366d9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Deliver requested project changes and check evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing codebase, evidence and execution results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select workspace actions and decide the resulting work artifact or report. - Decision owner: The configured builder CLI agent running Duo-issued tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role assignment, workspace, handoff storage, tasks and receipt checks. - Closure path: The builder acts on the workspace, reports outputs and checks, and receives review findings for subsequent revision. - Boundary reachability: The installed full and quick workflow ships the builder role, prompt, launch and returned artifact path. The external CLI supplies the configured executor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the builder the pipeline still routes requests but cannot choose or perform project changes. - Evidence: README.md, engine/handoff.py, engine/session.py - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate independent project agents&#39; contention over overlapping working trees. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two active runs or a remaining background job can work over the same tree. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose response to a competing workspace commitment. - Decision owner: The local run pool implements an overlap-specific admission relation. An autonomous coordination owner must be composed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Saved-run and live-actor registry, overlap checks and active-run constraints. - Closure path: A conflicting start is refused until the conflicting work is stopped or complete, or until a disjoint workspace is used. - Boundary reachability: The shipped engine applies the workspace check to ordinary local project run operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The project agents may act independently on their tasks. The admission disposition comes from fixed policy and caller construction of the schedule. - Distinct S1 units: Separately launched Duo project runs with autonomous builders. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent runs or leftover background jobs may write an overlapping workspace. - Attenuating coordination relation: The supplied admission check finds path overlap against live and saved background work before allowing a start. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: An attempt is held out of the overlapping workspace until the conflicting commitment ends or a disjoint workspace is selected. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Admission uses actual path overlap and reservations to prevent cross-run write interference. - Evidence: README.md, engine/runpool.py, engine/server.py - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate whole-run commitments when goal evidence is inadequate or the run reaches a decision point. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Reported completion can leave outstanding obligations or time constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide whether remaining whole-run work should be reassessed, resumed, revised or stopped. - Decision owner: Goal critic in the automated goal-loop mode. The local operator owns the distinct supervised plan and budget decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal-report parser, run state, pause function, updated round limits and dispatch. - Closure path: Goal judgment triggers reassessment, a revise turn, a blocker decision or closure. In supervised mode the run pauses for the owner and returns to execution under the changed plan constraints or budget. - Boundary reachability: The local engine supplies both goal-loop evaluation and owner-facing decisions in the same shipped run controls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model critic decides whether the overall goal is met. In the distinct supervised path the engine acts on the operator&#39;s returned current decision. - Whole-system current view: Run state contains the overall goal, active phase, documented results, saved checks, worker roles and round budget. - Current-control decision scope: Whether current commitments remain open and how to restart, constrain or fund the active workflow. - Evidence: engine/session.py, engine/session.py, engine/session.py, engine/session.py, engine/server.py - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Audit a builder&#39;s completion and quality claims against the underlying changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A build reply and its reported checks can omit defects or unfinished work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Render an independent code verdict and specify the required repairs. - Decision owner: The fresh critic agent in the full/selected quick-review path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Baseline snapshot, generated diff, separate review workspace, alternate provider preference and verdict parser. - Closure path: The critic reads a patch and workspace evidence, returns approval or required corrections, and a revision cycle consumes the findings. - Boundary reachability: Duo&#39;s full role workflow installs the review route and collects the isolated review outputs. The reviewed source provides this mode with one or multiple vendor CLIs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The critic selects defects and disposition. The engine copies evidence and enforces transitions. - Claim being audited: Builder reports that the implementation and its checks satisfy the task. - Ordinary reporting path: Build replies, receipts and generated handoff briefs. - Complementary access path: Workspace evidence and a diff against the runtime&#39;s recorded baseline. - Independence boundary: A fresh review session and scoped review copy reduce dependence on builder&#39;s preceding context. A distinct provider is preferred where available. - Who acts on findings: The runtime uses the verdict and defects to schedule subsequent builder revision or proceed. - Evidence: README.md, engine/session.py, engine/session.py, engine/phases.py, engine/roleflow.py - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. - Confidence: high - Caveats: Independence is relative to the builder&#39;s output and session. Different vendors are optional.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Use evidence of operator needs to change the local harness&#39;s future capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requests or gaps in previous behavior motivate a method or product change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select whether a tested local capability proposal becomes the active core. - Decision owner: Local installation owner for reviewed core proposals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Queued proposal worktree, agent implementation, test and review checks, and apply/tag mechanics. - Closure path: A proposal launches an isolated improvement run. On approval the tested change is integrated locally and loaded on the next launch. - Boundary reachability: The optional proposal route and self-improvement worker are present in the engine and UI server. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents implement and evaluate proposed changes. The core-approval action supplies the decisive return into future operation. - External distinction: The operator brings demand from using Duo on projects. - Future / prospective distinction: The improvement changes behavior of subsequent work rather than repairing the present project artifact. - Adaptation option generated: A concrete tested code proposal in a separate worktree. - Path back into current capability / S3: Owner approval cherry-picks the proposal and tags the change, loaded on next launch. - Evidence: engine/server.py, engine/selfdev.py, engine/selfdev.py - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. - Confidence: medium - Caveats: The assessed parent path is the core proposal workflow. Ordinary model discovery and a feature feedback report alone do not establish adaptation.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Ultimate identity governance was sought for a local installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential conflicts over purpose or ultimate policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level issue-return path established. - Decision owner: The owner sets practical local rules and accepts code changes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Settings, per-run constraints, versioned core and local proposal approval. - Closure path: Configuration changes apply to project work or later launches. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The supplied decisions regulate project constraints and reviewed updates. No first-party identity dispute reaches a standing ultimate authority and returns as an authoritative policy decision. - Evidence: README.md, engine/session.py, engine/server.py, engine/selfdev.py - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cd08c6eeafc60464eac5cf475fbd50fe426ac611/assessments/du/duo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cd08c6eeafc60464eac5cf475fbd50fe426ac611\">cd08c6e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-27T17:02:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Nabwinsaud/forgeline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Forgeline</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-27\">2026-11-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Nabwinsaud/forgeline/tree/2a2d2b7d9ffcb7183f7b77261eb1f54a1fd729ad\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2a2d2b7d9ffcb7183f7b77261eb1f54a1fd729ad\">2a2d2b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Implement and validate project changes in the installed factory. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incomplete specifications, source and test failures around a ticket. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the edits, tests and scoped changes needed for the assigned work. - Decision owner: Supplied model-backed builders and other operational roles. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role briefs, host model tools, job worktree and persisted result contract. - Closure path: Selected actions modify or inspect project state, verify outcomes, and return status/data consumed by the engine. - Boundary reachability: The first-party headless default runs a per-task Claude session, and the plugin skill invokes supplied subagents through the host without user assembly of a control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents own productive actions within the worktree. The host runs tools and the engine applies protected effects. - Evidence: Claude Code stage loop, Claude worker invocation, role results and group execution, stage result aggregation and return, builder role - Basis: Pinned executable wiring and supplied role definition. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Model providers and target repositories were not used at runtime.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate contention among independent ticket jobs and between roles that would write the same job worktree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent jobs can contend for workers and overlapping project files. Builder roles within a job share a writable tree. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Supply stage claims, isolation, writer ordering and bounded worker admission. - Decision owner: The first-party factory constructor supplies the coordination rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Job-specific worktrees, serialized build/test role groups, distinct stage-attempt result files and bounded thread pool. - Closure path: The launcher executes write groups in order, consumes results, then advances another stage or job. Independent worktrees keep unmerged effects separate. - Boundary reachability: The normal runner honors the pipeline group&#39;s parallel flag and processes exact result files. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Coordination is first-party and function-specific. The deployed runner follows configured relationships. - Evidence: job worktree setup, stage and attempt claims, role results and group execution, headless dispatch and job capacity, sequential write stages - Basis: Pinned interference mechanism and return. - Confidence: High for one launcher. - Caveats: Persisted stage lock records were not treated as proof of atomic multi-launcher arbitration. - Distinct S1 units: Independent ticket job agents and backend/frontend writers sharing an individual job&#39;s output branch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Parallel jobs can overwrite shared local files or overload workers. In-job writers can touch the same worktree. - Attenuating coordination relation: Per-job worktrees and bounded worker groups, with serialized write-capable stages inside a job. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Completed role outputs unlock the next stage. Conflicted or failed outcomes shape subsequent attempts. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The separation and ordering respond to concrete shared-file and execution-resource interference.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate a current job commitment across implementation, verification, review and release. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different operating roles may report incompatible evidence, a prior risk judgment can become invalid, or a job may be unable to advance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose to proceed, return work for repair or suspend the commitment pending higher-level judgment. - Decision owner: The installed approver model in the ordinary release path. The human operator in the separate paused-job mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Stage status aggregator, risk and gate-on-head enforcement, paused state and recorded operator action. - Closure path: Approver verdicts go to merge, rebuild or operator hold. The parent can reject, resume or select the retry stage, after which execution uses the saved decision. - Boundary reachability: The default job graph installs the same approver as a callable role and the CLI has a first-party paused-job control entry. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent judgment affects the job across roles. Code applies the transition. The human operator owns the different exception-mode judgment. - Evidence: approver&#39;s current release judgment, review and release transition rules, stage result aggregation and return, human intervention and re-entry, current factory status, merge policy and scoped override - Basis: Pinned evidence-to-current decision and distinct parent return. - Confidence: High within one coordinated job. - Caveats: This grade does not imply autonomous reallocation or reprioritization across all independent factory jobs. - Whole-system current view: The approver receives the whole current job&#39;s spec, current diff, gate results, risk and prior independent findings. The operator can inspect live job status and history. - Current-control decision scope: Send the job to correction, keep it on hold for a human, allow its current integration step, or in the parent mode abandon or retry an identified stage.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently examine the actual candidate change against its accepted specification and feed material discrepancies into current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Builder assertions or ordinary tests can miss requirements and harmful side effects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge whether a blocking discrepancy exists in the actual changes. - Decision owner: Factory&#39;s separate code-review and specialized reviewer roles. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role-scoped context, direct Git diff and source reads, typed findings and stage precedence. - Closure path: Blocking findings redirect the current job to build and are included in the next brief. Findings and gate results also reach the final approver. - Boundary reachability: The default pipeline automatically launches separate review roles on the proposed branch, with supported role-specific evidence access and structured feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewers make their own artifact-grounded judgment. No model-vendor distinction is needed. - Evidence: independent diff review role, Claude worker invocation, review and release transition rules, stage result aggregation and return, rebuild feedback, approver&#39;s current release judgment - Basis: Pinned review material, independent context and corrective return. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Role prescriptions and the headless tool selections are source evidence. They are not a claim that every host mode enforces complete read-only isolation. - Claim being audited: The current proposed branch implements the accepted criteria and is suitably tested. - Ordinary reporting path: Builder/test result JSON and the deterministic gate. - Complementary access path: A different dispatched role reads the full diff against base and surrounding source, and can examine tests independently. - Independence boundary: Separate review assignment and model invocation with no authorship of the candidate change, using direct repository evidence. - Who acts on findings: The engine routes blocking findings to builders; the final approver checks that earlier findings are resolved.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No established model-to-future-capability adaptation loop for the factory itself. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tickets and project environments can change the work demanded of the factory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied judgment over future factory capabilities with a returned adoption decision. - Decision owner: No qualifying runtime S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Initial domain setup and tracker intake sense different aspects of the environment. - Closure path: The live loop allocates work to existing roles. Setup configures a new factory without a recurring prospective decision path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Product change work and bootstrap stack detection are supplied as S1 and construction surfaces. - Evidence: domain setup, Claude Code stage loop, headless dispatch and job capacity, stack detection and checks generation - Basis: Bounded absence over setup, runtime transitions and role boundaries. - Confidence: Moderate. - Caveats: The operator can later edit domain setup using external judgment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No supplied closure over ultimate identity or policy for the installed factory. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Operating roles may encounter business-risk, safety and protected-path choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No escalated identity-level issue is decided and returned as a persisting policy decision for later jobs. - Decision owner: The external domain owner defines settings. The installed operator decides current job exceptions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Protected domain files, predefined rights, task-specific merge approval and deterministic guard. - Closure path: A job override is stored for that job and cannot resolve a persistent identity-policy conflict by itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The organization starts from human-authored domain and merge policy. Its live human command resolves a current case. - Evidence: domain setup, protected configuration, human intervention and re-entry, merge policy and scoped override - Basis: Bounded absence across authored policy and live operator surfaces. - Confidence: Moderate. - Caveats: Maintainer policy and project governance lie outside the factory&#39;s live loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4733750914d9aae8288343e67b1f47d0d9379b80/assessments/fo/forgeline.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4733750914d9aae8288343e67b1f47d0d9379b80\">4733750 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-27T16:46:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/deeeeone/Anvil\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Anvil</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-27\">2026-11-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/deeeeone/Anvil/tree/276aa0fcf2bb4bf31dd1940b1b71935d9e0c7754\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 276aa0fcf2bb4bf31dd1940b1b71935d9e0c7754\">276aa0f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out coding work and verify scoped changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repository uncertainty, tool failure and incomplete implementation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next supported action and corrections. - Decision owner: The model controlling a shipped session, within operator-selected scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool parser, permission policy, checkpoint and result transport. - Closure path: Run model-selected tools, return results into the next turn and report outcome or iterate. - Boundary reachability: An installed session constructs the loop and the Auto-edit/Bypass paths avoid universal human selection of ordinary actions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Human grants and gates constrain actions while the agent retains task-level discretion. - Evidence: run loop, action and feedback, installed session wiring, permission ownership - Basis: Pinned runtime code. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: No provider was called during the assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent independent coding sessions from overwriting each other&#39;s unmerged work or competing without limits for background capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Queue tasks and child sessions can target overlapping project paths or occupy the same background slots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Supply an explicit cross-session isolation, ordering and conflict-handling relation. - Decision owner: The installed constructors define worktree isolation and automatic slot policy. A parent model separately owns the incident intervention assessed in S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git worktrees, shared admission cap and merge conflict checks. - Closure path: A session waits for capacity, commits isolated work and exposes conflicts or completion before the other branch can enter the root. - Boundary reachability: Both queue and child sessions use shipped worktrees and the same capacity gate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The supplied inter-session protocol is rule-bound. The separate parent correction channel is current-control authority. - Evidence: background commitment control, worktree and capacity wiring, merge conflict and return - Basis: Pinned contention and enforcement paths. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: S2 is supported independently of any architectural claim about recursion. - Distinct S1 units: Independently executing child coding sessions and separately queued repository tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Overlapping edits and simultaneous consumption of the background slot pool. - Attenuating coordination relation: Separate worktrees, bounded background admissions and explicit merge conflict reports. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A waiting session starts when capacity frees; a conflict can return to its author as a rebase request. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The mechanisms change what happens when two independently acting work units contend for project state or execution capacity.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Control current commitments across work units in a parent-run team or the operator-supervised background portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Blocked or divergent work, merge conflicts and unwanted queue commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Redirect a child, order integration, hold or integrate its contribution, or cancel a background commitment. - Decision owner: Root model for the scoped team; project owner for the distinct queue supervision mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Parent-only tools, queue state, isolated branches, capacity setting and cancellation. - Closure path: Reports and conflicts reach the parent; follow-up messages start or steer the child, and a merge changes the integration state. Queue cancellation stops or drops the selected commitment. - Boundary reachability: Shipped SessionController injects the functions and the installed queue feeds operator decisions back to the same dispatcher. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The root model can independently act on child deviations in the enabled mode. The alternative queue intervention is owned by the human. - Evidence: installed session wiring, sub-session tools, merge conflict and return, queue view and cancellation, background commitment control, shared capacity setting - Basis: Pinned discretionary rights, intervention return and two supported closures. - Confidence: High for root mode; moderate for operator portfolio boundary. - Caveats: The deterministic dispatcher alone is not an S3 owner and the headless mode is not used for the parent-mode claim. - Whole-system current view: The agent sees the state, reports and messages of its child team. The operator can inspect project queue states, branches and session progress before acting. - Current-control decision scope: The root selects child corrections and integration order. The project owner cancels pending or running background commitments and sets capacity used by subsequent dispatch.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Check completion claims through a separate model read of produced evidence and return concrete contradictions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coding session may report completion despite a missing requirement or bugs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge whether the actual diff, file text and goal justify the claim. - Decision owner: The first-party side-call reviewer or completion judge supplies the judgment. The root agent and bounded loop act on it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Checkpoint originals, direct reads of current files and a fresh one-shot model context. - Closure path: Findings return as a new observation to the root and can reopen work or appear as unresolved at the bound. - Boundary reachability: The installed done path invokes the reviewer or configured judge and reads workspace files directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit sees actual changes in a separate prompt and can contradict the producing session&#39;s answer. - Evidence: review feedback path, actual file and review input, one-shot context - Basis: Pinned runtime read and correction path. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Coverage is limited to applicable requests and bounded file context. Some reviewer failures or exhausted budgets allow a reported limitation. - Claim being audited: The session completed the requested code change and satisfied its requirements. - Ordinary reporting path: The model&#39;s done reply and ordinary tool/build feedback. - Complementary access path: Checkpoint-to-current-file diffs and direct reads of the changed files plus the goal. - Independence boundary: A fresh one-shot context makes the judgment from workspace evidence without inheriting the producing interaction. - Who acts on findings: The producing root receives the findings and can perform further corrections and checks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No supplied external-and-prospective adaptation cycle is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider availability, task failures and changed repository needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No returned prospective capability decision found. - Decision owner: No qualifying S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Remote inventory feeds the picker and fallback reacts to a present failure. - Closure path: The observed paths return available choices or recover a session. They do not produce prospective adaptations evaluated for the project organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Provider discovery, installation, reactive retry and future task intake are separately implemented. - Evidence: provider inventory, run loop, installed session wiring, provider install surface, background commitment control - Basis: Bounded absence across capability surfaces. - Confidence: Moderate. - Caveats: Fallback can persist a session model without establishing prospective adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime ultimate identity/policy decision loop was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes to project boundaries, acceptable effects or permission assumptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No ultimate issue and returned governance resolution established. - Decision owner: The operator sets configurations and external maintainers own release decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission modes, explicit grants and per-project dispatch settings. - Closure path: Scoped approvals and settings operate without an identity-level escalation and binding return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A plan approval or permission setting concerns current project work or operating scope. - Evidence: supplied modes, permission ownership, shared capacity setting, installed session wiring - Basis: Bounded absence under installed app and operator modes. - Confidence: Moderate. - Caveats: Operator control over resources and preferences remains visible.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c6e8dd9a0e501e93d94cb72766c7562fb351b43f/assessments/an/anvil.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c6e8dd9a0e501e93d94cb72766c7562fb351b43f\">c6e8dd9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-28T02:12:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/thoufeeque22/zeroburn\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ZeroBurn</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-28\">2026-11-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/thoufeeque22/zeroburn/tree/5f4f3fc8991677d0bd3ceb8ef01f2ee3d08e6d2d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5f4f3fc8991677d0bd3ceb8ef01f2ee3d08e6d2d\">5f4f3fc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Implement scoped changes and produce a checked workspace artifact for a ticket. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Variable project changes, tool and test results, and remediation from prior stages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select implementation and verification actions and adapt them to results within the work scope. - Decision owner: The active model-backed session or registered development role in the supported host. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace plugin registration, role brief, configured tools, artifact paths and verification wrappers. - Closure path: The development agent reads the blueprint, selects edits and tests, acts on results and returns the development report. A returned review can drive another work turn. - Boundary reachability: The first-party distribution ships its registered development role, tool selection, evidence obligations and workflow dispatch to a compatible workspace host. The host supplies the underlying model/turn machinery. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the worker leaves execution hooks and checks but removes the discretionary choice of code changes and corrective actions. - Evidence: .agents/AGENTS.md, .agents/plugins/zeroburn/agents/dev-agent.md, .agents/base/ORCHESTRATION.md - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. - Confidence: high - Caveats: The positive state is scoped to the documented plugin-capable host. The standalone benchmark and generic script wrappers do not supply this loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Reduce concurrent write interference when operational workers share a workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel ticket slices can propose incompatible edits to one path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allocate or revise write scopes and respond to conflicting file claims. - Decision owner: The installed file-lease mechanism exposes a coordination-specific control path. Binding the worker identity and lease lifetime needs host-side composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party lease claim/release functions and write hook. - Closure path: A competing claim fails or the edit hook denies a conflicting write. The caller can then change scope, wait or release the claim. - Boundary reachability: The orchestration distribution directs the optional shared-workspace mode to supply lease scopes and worker IDs to the bundled guard. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The guard follows the configured file owner. Setup and composition determine who can make allocation decisions and how rejected work returns. - Distinct S1 units: Parallel builders independently implementing ticket slices in one workspace. - Inter-S1 disturbance: An edit by one worker can overwrite or conflict with another worker&#39;s planned changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: Path-specific claims and the pre-tool guard deny conflicting writes for an identified worker. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Rejected claims or edits require rescoping or waiting before the worker can continue on that path. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The relation directly targets concurrent writes to the same file rather than moving tasks among agents. - Evidence: .agents/base/ORCHESTRATION.md, .agents/scripts/repodigest.py, .agents/hooks/pre-tool-guard.mjs - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Advisory file state is a read/write mechanism, without an atomic claim guarantee. Enforcement is conditional on the host hook and worker identity.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate active ticket commitments, including splitting scope, skipping completed work and deciding what stage can proceed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Discovery can change blast radius, a resumed branch can already contain work, and audit can invalidate completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide which current stages and slice commitments are needed and whether to hold or redirect work. - Decision owner: The coordinating host agent under the installed workflow, and the human owner in the distinct escalation mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Resume gate, blueprint and stage reports, workflow return flags and human gates. - Closure path: The orchestrator uses current work and audit findings to select stages and redirect remediation. Unresolved scope or exhausted audit iterations return to the owner for the decision governing progression. - Boundary reachability: The bundled orchestrator instructions and audit workflow apply within the standard staged host mode and describe the returned operator actions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The orchestrator chooses within the allowed paths based on current evidence. In the supervised path the owner decides whether outstanding scope or results permit continued work. - Whole-system current view: Ticket, current branch state, blueprint, stage artifacts, findings and outstanding slice boundaries. - Current-control decision scope: Current ticket/slice commitments, escalation under material scope changes and the remedy path before progression. - Evidence: .agents/base/ORCHESTRATION.md, .agents/base/ORCHESTRATION.md, .agents/scripts/parallel-audit.py - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Routine sequencing and fixed audit caps are support. Scope selection and escalation return are the credited decisions.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Examine implementation and verification claims directly from changes in the workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The builder&#39;s report and ordinary quality evidence can miss defects or understated change scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Identify material findings from a directly inspected diff and decide whether additional tests are required. - Decision owner: Separate auditor model, with deterministic checks and local review supplying other inputs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only role, diff/check inputs, structured auditor response and non-model routing of findings. - Closure path: The audit path emits verified findings and a test signal. The orchestrator routes remediation to development or to QA followed by development before a bounded re-audit. - Boundary reachability: The installed workflow invokes the bundled auditor to examine the actual work and supplies the disposition to the standard staged run. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer chooses and evaluates findings and the test request. The workflow combines sources and applies its fixed severity policy. - Claim being audited: The delivered project change satisfies the ticket scope and verification commitments. - Ordinary reporting path: Development report and QA artifacts from the producing workflow. - Complementary access path: Direct workspace diff and scoped source inspection, alongside the original blueprint and check results. - Independence boundary: Separate role and context, read-only on application source. It can use findings from other checks without relying on the producer&#39;s assertion. - Who acts on findings: The orchestration agent reopens remediation and may route new tests. Repeated material failure reaches the owner. - Evidence: .agents/scripts/parallel-audit.py, .agents/scripts/parallel-audit.py, .agents/scripts/parallel-audit.py, .agents/base/ORCHESTRATION.md - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. - Confidence: high - Caveats: The aggregate decision includes static and local findings. The cloud auditor is a discretionary contributor, and its verdict is not the sole input to the gate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Prospective adaptation of installed workflow rules and model-specific workarounds. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Runtime and model changes can make installed practices stale across future tickets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and apply a change to the installed rule set based on a prospective assessment. - Decision owner: The bundled system role proposes edits and refers to owner approval. The end-to-end supported evaluation route is uncertain. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Expiry tags, optional maintenance triggers, release detection and an owner-invoked update utility. - Closure path: The role describes a proposed rule diff, approval and subsequent edit. A host-specific prospective audit dependency and return into subsequent effective operating rules were not established from this revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The installed role supplies a concrete candidate path, but evidence does not settle whether a complete function-specific adaptation loop is available in the declared host setup. - Evidence: .agents/base/ORCHESTRATION.md, .agents/plugins/zeroburn/agents/system-agent.md, .agents/hooks/session-start.mjs, .agents/scripts/init.mjs - Basis: Structural evidence with unresolved host integration. -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Ultimate identity or delegation governance was sought in the workspace plugin. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting mandates between target project, plugin and host. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied route for a last-resort purpose or identity decision. - Decision owner: Owner configures the workspace and gives operational permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static invariant and role files, scoped approval gates and configuration. - Closure path: Operating limits and approved maintenance edits affect work within the existing mandate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The system role is directed to preserve the invariants. The documented owner gates address current work and accepted maintenance. - Evidence: .agents/AGENTS.md, .agents/base/ORCHESTRATION.md, .agents/plugins/zeroburn/agents/system-agent.md - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1b39241e9d3030c48c0359dd56961d8bb8049bff/assessments/ze/zeroburn.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1b39241e9d3030c48c0359dd56961d8bb8049bff\">1b39241 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T19:11:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/2118505078xyh1/argentgo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">argentgo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/2118505078xyh1/argentgo/tree/0230ad0cff78aee5c97d182b62f753e2fdb85d71\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0230ad0cff78aee5c97d182b62f753e2fdb85d71\">0230ad0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform coding tasks through repeated model and workspace interactions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain files, failures and action effects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose tools, decide how to use results, and report the outcome. - Decision owner: Model-backed root or child agent carrying an operational task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party tool dispatch, checkpoints and transcript persistence. - Closure path: Tool results enter the conversation and feed subsequent model steps until a terminal output. - Boundary reachability: The ordinary CLI runs this agent loop with the shipped tools, and opted-in subagents use child runs from the same implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model discretion removes the operative coding decisions. - Evidence: internal/agent/loop.go, internal/agent/dispatch.go, internal/agent/dispatch.go, internal/app/appexecute.go. - Basis: Explicit - Confidence: High - Caveats: External providers supply inference. No live calls were made.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent interference between operative children sharing one checkout. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent children can edit the same files, and later work can depend on earlier output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Assign file ownership and decide which children can run in the same wave, then resolve observed conflicts. - Decision owner: The model-backed orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Packaged orchestrator prompt, task dispatch, and separate child context with explicit file scopes. - Closure path: Wave returns expose conflicts and dependencies. The lead changes the following assignment and retries or revises bounded work. - Boundary reachability: A workspace persona activates the first-party orchestrator prompt and task tool in the standard application execute path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses scopes, wave membership and conflict handling. The dispatcher executes these choices. - Evidence: internal/agent/prompts/orchestrator.txt, internal/app/appexecute.go, internal/agent/subagent.go, internal/agent/dispatch.go. - Basis: Explicit - Confidence: High - Caveats: This is prompt-directed regulation without hard worktree isolation. It applies in the enabled persona mode. - Distinct S1 units: Separate subagent runs with concrete operative subtasks and their own run contexts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Simultaneous edits by different child workers can collide in the shared workspace. - Attenuating coordination relation: The packaged lead must put file-overlapping work in successive waves and assign each child concrete file scope. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The lead consumes results and conflicts at the wave boundary and revises or reassigns the following wave. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party wave constraint is targeted to the same-file interference mechanism and requires corrective follow-through.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Control the present workload of one orchestrated request across its worker commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed subtasks and emerging dependencies can invalidate the active execution plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reprioritize or replace current commitments at wave boundaries based on the complete returns. - Decision owner: The lead model in the configured orchestrator mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task scope ledger in the lead conversation, child callbacks, shared execution and bounded dispatch. - Closure path: The lead sees finished workers and failures and changes the next assignment or dispatches a revised retry. - Boundary reachability: The stock prompt is injected by the standard opted-in persona setup and drives the shipped task return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead assesses present work and selects a new trajectory. Static child budgets and limits do not make this choice. - Evidence: internal/agent/prompts/orchestrator.txt, internal/agent/subagent.go, internal/agent/dispatch.go, internal/app/appexecute.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: High - Caveats: The claim covers decision points between work waves. It does not imply fleet-wide control or active cancellation of arbitrary external jobs. - Whole-system current view: Within a bounded request, the leader chooses and observes all synchronous wave commitments, with shared wave results and failure returns in its turn context. - Current-control decision scope: Defer or advance dependent work and decide what to retry, replace or leave out across the remaining operating plan.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Cross-check mutated workspace work through an independently invoked configured verification command and return corrective feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The editing agent&#39;s tool success or narrative may diverge from build or test evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine which direct check supplies the validity judgment for the particular codebase. - Decision owner: The operator or project supplies the independent check and criteria through the supported configuration. First-party runtime owns invocation and return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mutating batches trigger the opt-in verification callback. Workspace checks return exit status and bounded diagnostic evidence. - Closure path: A failure is sent into the editing session as a new correction turn or stops the run after the reflection limit. - Boundary reachability: The monitor path is an explicit first-party post-mutation configuration of the ordinary agent loop. A tool result alone cannot trigger the same feedback without the independent configured check. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate process supplies direct test/build evidence. The operator still selects the evidence and criteria. The standard separate task-verdict mode can judge only reported text unless its prompt requests independent evidence and the necessary tools are composed. - Evidence: internal/app/appexecute.go, internal/app/verifywire.go, internal/agent/verifyloop.go, internal/agent/subagent.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: Medium - Caveats: The verifier can be skipped if it cannot run. No autonomous primary-evidence assessor or hard always-on admission gate is claimed. - Claim being audited: The actual code changes remain valid against an operator-selected build or test criterion. - Ordinary reporting path: The editing run&#39;s tool outcome and its own generated report. - Complementary access path: A separately launched configured workspace check reads or tests the edited tree and supplies independent diagnostics. - Independence boundary: The command runs as its own process after the edited batch and reports the observed result. Domain-specific check selection and strength depend on local configuration. - Who acts on findings: The producing session receives a failure feedback turn. The runtime ends the run when its bounded corrective attempts run out.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Select prospective capability adaptations based on external intelligence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future provider, ecosystem or workload shifts may change suitable capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No in-boundary decision channel from external future evidence to capability adoption was established. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider probes and fallback, product update checks, runtime recovery and reusable skill authoring. - Closure path: Present provider status can switch the current request. An authorized written skill can be used subsequently. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party skill authoring path persists reusable procedures. The inspected path does not establish prospective external intelligence or an environmental adaptation decision over the whole assessed system. - Evidence: internal/agent/skillauthor.go, internal/agent/fallback.go, internal/provider/healthprobe.go, docs/USERGUIDE.md. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Set which standing operator and project instructions may govern the local coding organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Workspace-provided instructions can alter the standing operational mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select the trusted project instruction boundary and supply standing instructions. - Decision owner: The local operator authorizing project resources for the run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project trust setting, resource filtering and system prompt assembly. Enabled child persona instructions can be inherited inside the team. - Closure path: The permitted instruction sources enter the model system prompt and inherited instruction snapshot for subsequent operations. - Boundary reachability: The shipped CLI exposes project trust, and application construction applies it when including instruction resources in the system prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator controls which boundary can supply governing project instructions. The model carries the applied mandate. - Evidence: internal/app/trustenv.go, internal/app/appprompt.go, internal/memory/boundary.go, internal/cli/flagsusage.go, internal/agent/subagent.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: Medium - Caveats: Tool permission choices are narrower than this evidence. The S5 claim is confined to owner-governed standing instruction authority in this deployment. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Whose standing instructions can define the local agent&#39;s operative mandate at the workspace boundary. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The operator supplies standing instructions and authorizes the project-local instruction boundary. - Return-to-operation path: Resource filtering applies the decision when constructing the system prompt and worker instruction snapshot, which govern subsequent model actions.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8a19535bf6769126b18a7b89f9505e29c5b58323/assessments/ar/argentgo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8a19535bf6769126b18a7b89f9505e29c5b58323\">8a19535 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T19:00:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aswin-kevin/orchestrix\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orchestrix</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aswin-kevin/orchestrix/tree/34447e81f54a605cb780d534d8557573add273a3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 34447e81f54a605cb780d534d8557573add273a3\">34447e8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Deliver operator or host tasks through a model-driven tool loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain work inputs and tool outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and revise tool actions and end the task. - Decision owner: The run&#39;s model-backed agent, including coordinator workers executing operative subtasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool routing, permission enforcement, events and checkpointing. - Closure path: Tool results return to the transcript and directly condition subsequent model decisions. - Boundary reachability: Both the CLI and SDK run the first-party loop. The coordinator starts workers through the included subagent and async implementations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime supplies model and tools, while the model chooses how to carry out the task. - Evidence: agent/loop.go, agent/schedule.go, middleware/subagents/launcher.go. - Basis: Explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interference among worker assignments and incompatible worker outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel agents do not share the coordinator&#39;s context, and their outputs can overlap or contradict each other. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Partition independent assignments and revise work against conflict found in returned outputs. - Decision owner: The model-backed coordinator of the active team. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Default coordination prompt, bounded async slots, task/results registry and live task updates. - Closure path: The coordinator checks gaps or contradictions, revises worker instructions or sends follow-up work, then consumes the revised result. - Boundary reachability: The first-party coordinator constructor installs the default prompt and routes its model to the shipped task tools. Hosts supply the roster, not the coordination callback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The stock model supervisor owns the content of the assignments and how to resolve contradictions. A concurrency cap by itself does neither. - Evidence: coordinator/prompt.go, coordinator/coordinator.go, middleware/async/tools.go, middleware/async/notify.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: Medium - Caveats: This is prompt-mediated regulation in the documented multi-worker coordinator mode. It does not serialize worker edits to a shared backend. - Distinct S1 units: Separately launched worker model runs with their own task context. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Overlapping scopes or incompatible results can prevent the assigned workers from jointly delivering the requested output. - Attenuating coordination relation: The coordinator assigns independent scopes before parallel execution and uses results and contradiction checks to revise bounded worker assignments. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Updated instructions enter a running worker before its next step or become a new worker task, with the revised result returned to the coordinator. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The packaged prompt binds assignment partition and returned contradictions to a corrective worker step. The generic task tools alone do not establish the relationship.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate current work commitments and worker capacity for the whole request-scoped team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New dependencies, unnecessary running work and incomplete worker commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose whether to revise, cancel or start worker commitments across the current team. - Decision owner: The model-backed coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Thread-scoped task registry, list/check/update/cancel tools and before-stop progress reminder. - Closure path: Status and completion update the lead&#39;s current view. Cancel stops a selected run and returns its disposition; updates change subsequent worker steps. - Boundary reachability: The shipped coordinator composes the same async implementation that exposes the current task list and interventions to the model. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead exercises scope and continuation discretion with the runtime enforcing the selected action. - Evidence: coordinator/prompt.go, middleware/async/prompt.go, middleware/async/tools.go, middleware/async/records.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: High - Caveats: The current view is confined to the assigned coordinator thread. No claim is made for unrelated top-level runs or a distinct whole-team human supervisory mode. - Whole-system current view: Within the assessed team, listtasks refreshes the full thread-scoped background task set. The lead also holds its synchronous work and result history. - Current-control decision scope: Change current commitments through start, update and cancel over the workers assigned to the request, with observed status and outcomes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independently challenge operational claims through complementary evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A worker or lead may report an answer unsupported by actual work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No packaged audit-specific primary-evidence judgment path was established. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The coordinator reads reported worker results. The rubric checks the final answer against criteria and can trigger revision. - Closure path: Rubric feedback returns to the producing run as an answer revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The standard rubric sees task, criteria and answer. A custom grader or read-only reviewer can add access by supplying external policy and implementation. - Evidence: middleware/rubric/rubric.go, middleware/rubric/grader.go, coordinator/prompt.go, README.md. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Use external intelligence to choose a prospective capability adaptation for the team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future changes in models, tools, or workload can render the present capability mix unsuitable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped future-directed adaptation decision was established. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider fallback, model profiles, memory, and web tools support configured execution or task work. - Closure path: Fallback and model-specific profiles adjust current model use; memory can inform future tasks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Built-in profiles match configured models and fallback handles failures. Neither supplies a loop from external evidence and anticipated conditions to an adaptation choice. - Evidence: fallback.go, middleware/profiles/profiles.go, README.md. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Define the standing mandate and identity for the operator-launched agent or its configured SDK team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Possible mandates and role boundaries conflict with the operator&#39;s purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select the system mandate under which the top-level model and coordinator are commissioned. - Decision owner: The CLI operator or responsible parent application owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The CLI reads the operator&#39;s system prompt file and passes it to the agent constructor. The SDK coordinator accepts a host-owned prompt and worker roles. - Closure path: At runtime the loaded mandate is assembled into model requests and governs subsequent turns and how the lead commissions workers. - Boundary reachability: orx exposes the operator-owned mandate file directly. A host can also instantiate the first-party coordinator with its own governing prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent receives the mandate and works under it. The operator or parent owner chooses the standing purpose before commissioning the run. - Evidence: cmd/orx/main.go, coordinator/coordinator.go, options.go, agent/loop.go. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: Medium - Caveats: Per-tool approval does not establish S5. The positive claim concerns the first-party channel for assigning the enduring agent or team role. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which standing role and purpose the commissioned agent or team serves across its user work. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The launching operator or accountable host sets the mandate. No distinct agent-owned ultimate policy mechanism was established. - Return-to-operation path: The selected role is loaded into the constructed agent and presented to the model on each run. The model&#39;s decisions then execute under that mandate.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6607cd88a5d0d006b15c8fc8a9f865e40ba12801/assessments/or/orchestrix.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6607cd88a5d0d006b15c8fc8a9f865e40ba12801\">6607cd8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-28T02:03:53+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/T-Crypt/hadal\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hadal</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-28\">2026-11-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/T-Crypt/hadal/tree/b6eea00a3058491f185c0bcc76ac550caa54e098\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b6eea00a3058491f185c0bcc76ac550caa54e098\">b6eea00 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform workspace and coding tasks in an installed session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing project inputs, task requirements and tool outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose work actions and revise them from observations. - Decision owner: Model in the shipped model-backed Hadal session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Embedded Pi Durable runtime, registered tools, workspace execution environment and persisted commits. - Closure path: Session selects tools, observes results and continues until an answer or a runtime limit. - Boundary reachability: Hadal opens the durable runtime with its registered tools and execution environment in its supported self-hosted distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime persists and executes work. Removing the model removes the decisions about which work to perform. - Evidence: README.md, package.json, src/server/app.ts - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. - Confidence: high - Caveats: Model providers are configured dependencies. Package installation and live inference were not exercised.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate contention between independent sessions using different models on one GPU. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Alternating requests evict models and delay both independent agency loops. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a response to competing model use when the shared card cannot serve both models efficiently. - Decision owner: The shared model lane supplies an interference-specific regulation path. Composition would be needed for an autonomous owner to change its policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GPU model acquisition and release, turn-completion feedback and grouped admission. - Closure path: Pending model requests wait until a turn is available. A tool-use result can hold the current model for the continuation so queued work changes execution timing. - Boundary reachability: The app installs a first-party request wrapper for the supported llama-swap model path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lane executes a fixed prescription. It does not give a model authority to set cross-session resource arbitration. - Distinct S1 units: Independent work sessions and active subagents issuing requests for their own tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Contention for resident models on one local inference card causes frequent model swaps and interrupts ongoing loops. - Attenuating coordination relation: A model-aware shared-card lane preserves a running model across tool turns and waits to admit conflicting requests. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Tool-use completion can retain the lane, while release allows waiting sessions to acquire it and resume inference. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The lane regulates concrete interference from incompatible model residency across the operating sessions. - Evidence: docs/features.md, src/server/app.ts - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate active session commitments and resources on behalf of the local installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple live sessions can carry changing tasks, blocked approvals and spending demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose where to assign or steer currently authorized work and how to act on blocked or resource-constrained commitments. - Decision owner: Lantern model within standing or current delegated work. Owner remains the current budget and intervention authority in the supervised path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Current session inventory, session reads, steering, return reports, spend view and enforcement. - Closure path: Lantern reads the current sessions, changes a worker&#39;s current task or starts separate work and receives outcomes. The owner can receive the active status and change spend limits or intervene via the session control path. - Boundary reachability: Lantern is a bundled extension with tool registry integration. Its tool decisions and the owner&#39;s live controls both reach the installed sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses and issues an authorized steering response. A distinct parent path returns the owner&#39;s resource or intervention decision into ongoing work. - Whole-system current view: Lantern can inspect visible sessions, their current state and results. The owner sees Running now and spend across sessions. - Current-control decision scope: Select, redirect or hold current session commitments. The owner can apply live spend limits or act on blocked sessions. - Evidence: src/server/extensions/pip.ts, src/server/extensions/pip.ts, docs/features.md - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Lantern&#39;s authority is bounded by the owner&#39;s instructions. The app reserves some approvals, stops and model selection to people or explicit prior mandates.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Cross-check a router implementation&#39;s completion claims directly against code and tests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The implementation report can miss defects, failed edge cases or code paths affected by the change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide what underlying artifacts to inspect and which findings must be repaired before progression. - Decision owner: Fresh reviewer stage agent in the shipped standard router. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Router dispatch, fresh stage contexts, workspace tools, independent findings file and ordered stage controls. - Closure path: The reviewer inspects code and tests and records findings. The router sends the fix agent to those findings and re-runs review as needed. - Boundary reachability: The distribution includes the standard router with review and fix templates and the stage-specific subagent path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer independently selects the evidence and findings. The router uses them for current execution decisions. - Claim being audited: The implementation meets the plan and work requirements. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementation stage response and shared run artifacts. - Complementary access path: The review stage reads the actual diff, exercises tests and inspects callers. - Independence boundary: A fresh stage agent gets its own context. It may use the same model weights and reads the shared run folder. - Who acts on findings: The router assigns a fix stage and may run review again before progression. - Evidence: src/server/routers.ts, src/server/routers.ts, src/server/routers.ts - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. - Confidence: high - Caveats: The witness is the configured standard router mode. A plain chat does not run this reviewer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Keep future sessions aligned with durable changes in working environments and user practices. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repository conventions, work procedures or operator preferences can change between sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide whether a proposed cross-session lesson governs subsequent work. - Decision owner: Installation owner who approves, rejects or changes a scoped lesson. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Memory tool, pending lessons, review and exact-content approval, and prompt assembly. - Closure path: The session proposes durable changes from project or user evidence. The approval updates in-force memory reinserted into later sessions&#39; prompts. - Boundary reachability: The first-party memory extension and controls implement the proposal, owner decision and re-use in affected sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model detects and proposes prospective lessons. The owner has the decisive right to put a proposed change into force. - External distinction: Project documentation/conventions and operator requests encountered by the session. - Future / prospective distinction: A lesson is proposed for reuse beyond this task, at project or installation scope. - Adaptation option generated: An add, change or forget proposal for a specific lesson. - Path back into current capability / S3: Exact-content owner approval makes the changed lesson in force, and later prompts receive it. - Evidence: docs/features.md, src/server/extensions/memory.ts, src/server/memory.ts, src/server/memory.ts - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. - Confidence: high - Caveats: This finding is scoped to explicit prospective lessons. Transcript retention and operational notifications alone do not supply S4.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Ultimate mission or policy governance was sought at installation level. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting policy or identity demands across sessions or roles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No distinct ultimate-policy decision return identified in the reviewed release. - Decision owner: The owner configures the installation and holds selected operational permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session roles, trust controls, owner tool bounds, prompt rules and scoped memory approval. - Closure path: Ordinary permissions, settings and approved lesson changes affect subsequent decisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The inspected routes govern access, resources and future working procedures. They provide no distinct case of an identity-level dispute routed to ultimate authority and returned as a binding policy decision. - Evidence: docs/features.md, src/server/extensions/pip.ts, src/server/memory.ts - Basis: Structural evidence from pinned first-party source. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f44bb8b6176baf8be3d5f3b511b805b5a74e9ebc/assessments/ha/hadal.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f44bb8b6176baf8be3d5f3b511b805b5a74e9ebc\">f44bb8b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-19T21:45:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/morphllm/PenHarness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PenHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-19\">2026-11-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/morphllm/PenHarness/tree/83c540fa9fe9f65b75d949b44e9199f02eb2bea6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 83c540fa9fe9f65b75d949b44e9199f02eb2bea6\">83c540f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce evidence-graded authorized security investigation findings and reports through model-led task cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown target behavior/source, false hypotheses, failures and evidence that changes an investigation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next scoped investigation action, falsify a hypothesis and report a supported outcome. - Decision owner: configured lead/lane/worker models in the first-party installed composition; opencode supplies the external execution engine. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: opencode HTTP/CLI transport, packaged roles/skills/provider config, fresh sessions, fleet scripts, file artifacts and idle continuation. - Closure path: worker actions produce evidence/files; harvest and mailbox reports reach the lead, which chooses new task cells, correction or termination. - Boundary reachability: default opencode.json binds supplied skills/model, the plugin resumes prepared root sessions, and fleet bootstrap copies AGENTS/skills/plugin/config into workers before native fresh-session prompts. This is a shipped composition, not a claim about all opencode internals. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the models choose task-dependent investigations and evidence disposition; HTTP dispatch, JSON parsing and repeat ticks transport their choices. - Evidence: default provider/skill binding, installed lead doctrine, hunt workflow, idle continuation plugin, fleet lifecycle/roles/harvest, worker provider configuration - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: source-only assessment of the supplied workflow. No target, model, cloud fleet, credential or security test was used; prompt-based control is not a security-enforcement guarantee.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate overlapping investigation ownership, duplicate work and conflicting central-ledger writes among work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel lanes/workers can reopen already falsified hypotheses, duplicate probes and contend over shared findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose disjoint task ownership and revise/deduplicate proposed work against the shared proven/dead state before dispatch. - Decision owner: installed lead model and worker/lane models following the packaged coordination role; fixed queues/parser support those decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: one-topic ownership, lead-only central merge, per-worker artifacts, shared dead-end ledger, fresh-session identity, optional flock-backed task dequeue and project-local plugin filtering. - Closure path: workers/lanes inspect existing results before work; proposals and findings return through harvest/mailbox; lead deduplication and new disjoint dispatch change subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: lead doctrine, worker/lane prompts and bootstrap supply the specific anti-duplication/ownership loop; optional queue scripts add mechanical exclusive task claims. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses topic/ownership changes from actual shared evidence. Queue locks, single merge writer and session filters do not themselves own that choice; delegation alone is not the witness. - Evidence: installed lead doctrine, hunt workflow, fleet lifecycle/roles/harvest, queue worker, atomic queue append, idle continuation plugin - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: prose coordination and lead discipline are not a global atomic reservation guarantee. Main HTTP dispatch does not implement the optional shell queue; JSONL append/harvest are not certified race-free.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current hunt commitments and fleet capacity from collective health/progress/evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: idle/unhealthy workers, saturated lanes, changing priorities and continuing cloud cost across the active engagement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base: choose capacity, lane priorities, dispatch/reseed/steer and teardown; parent: inspect fleet status and exercise the same documented operator resource/commitment commands. - Decision owner: base: installed lead model. Parent mode: authorized engagement operator holding the local fleet controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fleet-state.json, status health/session/finding summaries, proposal/mailbox return, up/lane/dispatch/steer/harvest/down and TTL. - Closure path: lead loop reads current ledger/status/harvest and chooses further allocation/intervention; up/down actually provision/destroy workers, steer changes a live lane&#39;s next turn. Operator commands return durable fleet state/status and change active work. - Boundary reachability: the idle plugin explicitly asks the installed lead to inspect current state and select parallel next actions; fleet.py binds those choices to native provider/session actions. README documents manual CLI operation as another supported control mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model/operator owns resource/priority choices. TTL, round-robin placement, health probes and destruction APIs execute/enforce them; neither mere initial task decomposition nor a machine named lead establishes S3. - Evidence: idle continuation plugin, installed lead doctrine, hunt workflow, fleet lifecycle/roles/harvest, supported deployment/manual CLI - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A refers to the admitted lead composition, not an autonomous cloud account authority or externally hosted agent internals. P is local engagement operation, not project-wide governance.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: a concrete independent-verification instruction exists, but complementary audit ownership/closure cannot be reconstructed unambiguously. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a composer&#39;s proposed chain or reported evidence can be unsupported by the underlying observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: which separate actor owns a complementary claim-relative audit, rather than ordinary production proof/control generation, is unresolved. - Decision owner: not established for S3; lead, composers and workers are named but no distinct verifier authority is bound. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh worker sessions, raw proof/control artifacts, candidate queue and central ledger admission. - Closure path: workflow says composer output must not enter the ledger until independently verified and can become fleet queue work; source does not specify the verifying actor/evidence-selection independence sufficiently to establish positive audit or bounded absence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary worker controls and outward audits of the target are S1. The explicit independent composer-verification path is specific enough to prevent simply publishing absence, but generic fresh-session dispatch is not sufficient C/A evidence. - Evidence: hunt workflow, fleet lifecycle/roles/harvest, installed lead doctrine - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: No live trace is required by this assessment as a new threshold. The gap is source-level role/claim/independence/return interpretation of the shipped independent-verification step; separate contexts alone do not settle it.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supplied prospective adaptation loop for the harness&#39;s future organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new target/source/advisory facts alter the current investigation plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no harness-capability option selection and prospective adoption return established. - Decision owner: models choose investigation tasks/lanes; methodology/configuration changes remain developer/operator inputs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: external source reading, advisory lookup, current lane invention, evidence ledger/dead ends and fixed skill/config files. - Closure path: external facts grow the next hunt actions/chains, the primary product outcome; no first-party reusable capability/strategy evaluation and adoption loop alters the harness for future environments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: external reconnaissance and discovering new investigation branches are S1, not automatically S4; a skill authored from a past engagement is historical development evidence. - Evidence: hunt workflow, idle continuation plugin, installed lead doctrine, fleet lifecycle/roles/harvest, hunt state layout - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the target&#39;s possible future risks are part of the investigation product; they do not establish adaptation of this harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication and governing return established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: engagement scope, authorized targets/credentials, fixed red lines and publication permission constrain work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no explicit ultimate-policy issue-resolution path, as distinct from missing task authorization or credentials. - Decision owner: user writes initial scope and approves publication; static safety/mission rules come from the supplied role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scope/brief files, fixed red list, model instructions and plugin readiness/pause/blocked notes. - Closure path: missing scope/credentials causes planning/stop/notes; supplying task scope or ordinary permission does not implement an identity-policy dispute resolution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a written authorization file and final human permission are not sufficient S5 evidence. The plugin checks readiness, not legitimate policy authority. - Evidence: engagement boundary template, installed lead doctrine, hunt workflow, idle continuation plugin, hunt state layout - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: scope controls here are largely instruction-based, not a claim of comprehensive enforcement. No external target permission is inferred or exercised by this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c2e43bf4750841277b3e58d8ba80314121734764/assessments/pe/penharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c2e43bf4750841277b3e58d8ba80314121734764\">c2e43bf &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-08T15:26:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dhoelzer/AgentOrchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orchestrator Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-08\">2026-11-08</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dhoelzer/AgentOrchestrator/tree/33fce2cc0d0b3dd4dcd0e7267391b45a2d4edded\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 33fce2cc0d0b3dd4dcd0e7267391b45a2d4edded\">33fce2c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialists perform model-chosen work on project workspace files, commands and tracked deliverables; the root orchestrator is also a model-operated agent, but its regulatory decisions are credited under S2/S3 rather than double-counted as production operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concrete implementation/test/file problems and tool results encountered while completing assigned cards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the specialist model chooses tool actions and reports completion/partial/blocking evidence inside its bounded assignment. - Decision owner: model-backed specialist agent, through OpenHands SDK conversation and first-party tool wiring. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: terminal/file-editor/task-tracker SDK tools, project sandbox, concurrency/turn ceilings, message and event ledger. - Closure path: the specialist changes real project files and returns results to its conversation/parent; the board captures execution/review state, which triggers further activity. - Boundary reachability: the shipped root main.py constructs the runtime and orchestrator; RunAgentExecutor calls dispatch, which passes tools to AgentRuntime.run and records subsequent outcomes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action selection resides with the model-backed specialist; deterministic scheduler and SDK executor transport/enforce choices but do not pick the coding actions themselves. - Evidence: worker and orchestrator tool registry, runtime dispatch, SDK run. - Basis: structural source, supported startup and mocked tool/board tests. - Confidence: high for an installed agent-action path; no credentialed external-provider success run is claimed. - Caveats: OpenHands SDK and external LLM are required; their internal control planes are outside the first-party assessment boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: keep independently operating specialist tasks from simultaneously interfering on shared project files and blocked dependencies. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two otherwise parallel workers touching the same file or a downstream worker starting against unfinished upstream output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root model decides task separation/dependency edges and can re-plan task relationships from each fresh board/attention briefing; the scheduler subsequently enforces the chosen edges mechanically. - Decision owner: root orchestrator agent for the conflict-prevention plan; deterministic scheduler for execution of that model-authored plan. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependson task fields, sequential readiness, project-level slot cap, task statuses, generated briefings and tool-mediated updates. - Closure path: orchestrator chooses dependencies for overlapping work; scheduler refuses to start a dependent task until all predecessor cards are done; completion produces an unblock and new agent dispatch, while blocker/rework feedback allows the next orchestrator cycle to change the plan. - Boundary reachability: the root system prompt explicitly commands dependson when parallel tasks would touch the same files, CreateTaskExecutor stores model-chosen edges, and the shipping scheduler checks them on every sweep. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: conflict-sensitive choice of edges is a model tool call, not an authored static DAG. The runtime&#39;s bare dependency check is not independently S2=A, and evidence does not establish automatic real-time file-lock conflict detection. - Distinct S1 units: separately created project specialists, with each one owning a model/file workspace execution stretch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: named shared-file collision between parallel task workers, together with downstream use of unfinished upstream files. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-authored dependson serializes the interfering work while leaving unrelated cards eligible for concurrent execution. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: predecessor done releases a card to ready, next scheduler dispatch injects task/dependency notes and prior review feedback; root receives current board statuses for re-planning. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the supplied orchestrator prompt ties dependency choice to a particular inter-worker file collision, and the dependency gate actively controls conflicting worker start; generic task order alone would not support this claim. - Evidence: root planning rule, task edges/edit, dependency scheduler, dependency test. - Basis: explicit operating prompt, source-enforced dependency return, local stub tests. - Confidence: medium: the intended shared-file interference is explicit, but live model identification of correct dependency edges was not observed. - Caveats: no actual file-overlap detector or automatic cross-task lock. A model that omits needed edges can still cause collision; mere slot caps are not credited as the agent-owned S2 decision.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-project current board and regulate allocations, staffing, blocked recovery, review and delivery decisions across worker cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unstaffed work, spent budgets, project stalls, failed models, review/rework queues and imbalanced worker/endpoint capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root model chooses task decomposition, worker assignment, budget extension versus reconfiguration/splitting, current plan edits, reviewer dispatch and explicit delivery signal from new board feedback. - Decision owner: the continuously reused root orchestrator model/agent; human edits, pause and config controls impose constraints and may override but do not constitute the credited autonomous path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persisted Store board, detect() attention, cyclebriefing, createtask, assigntask, extendagent, reconfigureagent, retireagent, config reload, slot gate and deterministic Scheduler.sweep. - Closure path: issue/change/block appears in Store; the next root cycle receives a cross-card and capacity briefing, issues a chosen mutation through board tools, and tasks are dispatched/reviewed/blocked accordingly; later results return to that same Store and next-cycle decisions. - Boundary reachability: main.py launches the never-ending loop; runcycle checks live attention, runprojectcycle uses root SDK model and passes the root board tools, and scheduler runs both before and independently of the root cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: staffing and recovery choices disappear when the root model is removed. Automatic capacity accounting and status transition are supporting controller mechanics, not the entire S3 claim. - Whole-system current view: per-project board, dependencies, agents, blockers, reviewer status, cost/capacity and user messages in fresh root briefings, across the managed specialist S1 cells. - Current-control decision scope: project portfolio task and worker commitments, resourcing, rework direction and delivery timing; no claim that the model autonomously rewrites system-level policy/LLM infrastructure. - Evidence: attention/blocked detection, cycle and project control, orchestrator authority tools, root discipline. - Basis: shipped loop, named board decisions and tests of blocking/dispatch. - Confidence: high for architectural agent-owned project regulation. - Caveats: global cross-project cycle is sequential per project; no separate autonomous cross-domain S3 beyond the installed project portfolio is inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: a distinct reviewer agent independently inspects actual files/testable results against task acceptance criteria and returns a corrective verdict on worker output. - Disturbance / variety regulated: erroneous implementation or unsupported worker claims surviving routine completion reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in a configured reviewer mode, a separate model reviewer decides approved versus changesrequested after reading files; RecordReviewExecutor translates that judgment into done or rework and the root re-dispatches on corrective feedback. - Decision owner: a separate model reviewer when explicitly equipped with the recordreview tool; the orchestrator remains parent control of staffing/response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct reviewer task link, reviewer assignment prompt, read-file/terminal SDK tools, reconfigureagent, review rounds, feedback persistence and rework board transitions. - Closure path: worker finishes to review; parent creates/attaches reviewer; reviewer opens independent file read, checks criteria and records verdict; failing work moves to rework with review feedback in the next worker run. - Boundary reachability: default shipping tool registry permits a root to reconfigure a newly created reviewer with recordreview, then dispatch runagent(review=true); the tool writes verdict and rework state. However CreateSubAgentExecutor validates action.tools but fails to assign it to AgentSpec.toolsoverride, while the default worker tool set omits recordreview. The advertised one-step reviewer creation does not itself close a direct verdict. This is a composable, not reliably default mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct reviewer model rather than creator&#39;s same tool state can inspect actual files and choose a verdict; it needs explicit tool wiring, and default root self-inspection alone is not counted as independent S3. - Claim being audited: a worker&#39;s claim that named project deliverables satisfy task-specific acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: worker&#39;s reporttoparent and normal task completion/summary, which are not themselves independent proof. - Complementary access path: reviewer readfile/terminal reads workspace artifacts and may run tests independently from worker&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: different child agent/conversation and reviewer pointer from worker&#39;s assignedagentid; both share first-party project files, so no stronger sandbox/process isolation is inferred. - Who acts on findings: reviewer verdict switches board done/rework, with orchestrator/scheduler returning failure feedback to the next worker stretch. - Evidence: reviewer prompts, creation tool omission, reconfigure tool/registry, review submission, reviewer scheduler. - Basis: source-level executable mode and mocked board tests, no live reviewer provider run. - Confidence: medium for the explicitly equipped mode; low for author claims of out-of-box automatic reviewer finalization. - Caveats: reviewer creation&#39;s tools argument is presently a no-op at the pin, and reviewerprompt is defined but not automatically selected by CreateSubAgentExecutor. Default prompts alone must not be treated as verified independent adjudication.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate agent-owned environmental scanning, future-option generation and implemented organizational adaptation loop was identified in the installed distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential future tool/platform/model change would require a prospective response, but the observed scheduler chiefly handles current throughput and present user cards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for autonomous outside/then strategy; endpoint selection and automatic timeouts are current deterministic transport tuning. - Decision owner: user changes configured LLM servers and task goals; root agent replans current work but is explicitly told not to change harness configuration itself. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config reload, model endpoint router health signals, new user-input attention, arbitrary specialist prompts. - Closure path: no first-party agent-origin external trend/forecast option is chosen and carried into a durable future capability or strategy change. A user-set configuration may reload but is not such a loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: endpoint tuning and iterative project replanning are S1/S3 current-control support, not evidence of an independently closed S4 function. - Evidence: config reload, do not reconfigure rule, current-attention routing. - Basis: shipped model action inventory, operating prompt and selected state/endpoint paths. - Confidence: medium-high within specified first-party runtime modes. - Caveats: this is not a claim that human maintainers cannot plan future versions outside the harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate first-party ultimate identity/constitutional policy authority is exposed as a separately closed S5 decision process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing portfolio purposes or permanent policy contradictions would require legitimate identity-level arbitration, but observed choices are task/agent instructions and runtime configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced beyond task-level human direction and root-controlled specialist prompt/mission changes. - Decision owner: static installed root ORCHESTRATORSYSTEMPROMPT and the human software owner define core identity; the model can create/reconfigure subagent instructions but not set the harness&#39;s own ultimate policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: frozen system prompt, config fields, project scope, human UI pause/manual delivery, per-agent systemprompt overrides. - Closure path: no first-party policy dispute adjudicator with changed identity fed back into the root runtime; changes to child missions remain subordinate S3 control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a root&#39;s ability to author a new worker prompt is delegation/operational policy, not legitimate authority to rewrite its own constitutional identity; static operator settings do not imply a distinct parent S5 mode. - Evidence: fixed root identity, root spec creation, child prompts/reconfiguration. - Basis: first-party identity/prompt tooling search and source inspection. - Confidence: medium-high for absence in the supported runtime boundary. - Caveats: external repository governance by maintainers is not part of the installed agent application assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0a07f100b820ab800e685f36a8047b6cfc54d18d/assessments/or/orchestrator-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0a07f100b820ab800e685f36a8047b6cfc54d18d\">0a07f10 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T19:55:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/weixijia/mas-office\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MAS Office</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/weixijia/mas-office/tree/5a768ecac2260ba4cb21c5450593ad1b7d190041\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5a768ecac2260ba4cb21c5450593ad1b7d190041\">5a768ec &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform one delegated research/decision subtask by searching/fetching external information, writing scoped source cards and work outputs, and adapting to encountered evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting search results, variable source availability, ambiguous user criteria, incomplete facts and tool-access failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select research strategy, tools, evidence and a work artifact in a role&#39;s approved envelope rather than following a wholly fixed sequence. - Decision owner: Real model-backed claude-code or codex role session created under a first-party compiled job/brief; the model/provider&#39;s own internals are not credited as MAS Office-specific control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compileRole, decideTool, model adapter, tool allow/ask/deny and source-card output format, events/transcript, scoped writable files, stage task envelopes. - Closure path: Worker&#39;s model turn writes source/work files and returns summary; runtime records task completion/handoff and passes results to dependent checker and Lead; a rework request returns to the same role. - Boundary reachability: Standard mas-office up with a locked team and authenticated Claude Code/Codex CLI starts actual model sessions; no author-supplied custom adapter needed for the normal path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: First-party role composition creates a context-sensitive agent task with real model decisions; the scheduler and permission compiler enforce constraints but cannot select external evidence or conclusions by themselves. - Evidence: packages/runtime/src/engine/project.js; packages/runtime/src/backends/claude.js; packages/runtime/src/backends/codex.js; packages/node/src/team/compile.js; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate cross-role source/output conflicts and prevent premature/competing stage or tool execution across concurrently working S1 actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent agents trying to overwrite another role&#39;s results/instructions, stage consumers reading unfinished dependencies, competing access to shared process pool or unapproved tools. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple real parallel finder/researcher and reviewer role sessions in distinct logical work cells of one team; different model backends are supported. - Inter-S1 disturbance: One role could corrupt another&#39;s work/source path, read unready input, or compete for limited execution slots if allowed unconstrained shared writes and reads. - Attenuating coordination relation: compileRole assigns per-role writable paths, decideTool rejects out-of-scope writes before tool execution, staged runStage waits for preceding work/review before next group consumes results; process-wide semaphore bounds competing turns. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A denied foreign write returns to its actor as a policy refusal, and pending downstream stages start only after upstream work/verification has finished or resolved through human gate. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Writable-per-role exclusion is a concrete mutual-interference attenuator for independently active sessions, alongside dependency-staged admission; a generic event log or forwarder alone would not qualify. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine if an agent may modify a particular shared-workspace path and when mutually dependent role work is allowed to proceed. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic permission compiler, policy engine, role stage scheduler and shared semaphore; the Lead does not hold an autonomous function-specific anti-conflict decision right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Compiled role writable glob, deny rules, Claude/Codex sandbox permission checks, group ordering, per-project role completion and process parallelism limit. - Closure path: Scope violation is denied; in-bounds role produces its own artifact; dependency completion and reviewer results release the next stage&#39;s roles, avoiding cross-role destructive interference. - Boundary reachability: Bundled up installs and applies compiled roles and decideTool at runtime, including locked team inputs, without requiring a third-party S2 implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An actual S2-specific relation is implemented and shipped but current enforcement choices are fixed deterministic logic; no autonomous dedicated S2 controller observed. - Evidence: packages/node/src/team/compile.js; packages/node/src/team/decide.js; packages/runtime/src/engine/project.js; docs/design/runtime.md. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Coordinate and adapt present whole-run role briefs, stage commitments, stalled workers, reviewer returns, and the decision to publish/rework/stop a source-checked result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting/current tasks, unresponsive roles, checker objections, unavailable resources, user requirements and escalating bounded review rounds. - Whole-system current view: Model Lead receives team structure, user task, stage status, per-role summaries and handoffs, failures, reviewer findings, run checkpoints and global current work state. - Current-control decision scope: Lead chooses per-role briefs and response to stalls (retry, replan or ask-human), produces a whole-run plan/write-up and can direct work/revisions within the locked team; human parent decides blocking stage and plan/final gates, whether workers must redo work and whether to stop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose current task allocations in briefs/repair interventions and whether major current stage commitments advance or return. - Decision owner: Base: model-backed Lead session acting inside source-native run control; parent: authorized user acting on substantive plan/stage gate and checker objections via tokened inbox, not a generic shell permission prompt. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan(), runStage(), onStall(), runGate(), writeUp(), role lifecycle events, source checks, run state, human command broker. - Closure path: Lead&#39;s current briefs control future role turns; stalled role leads to retry/replan with new brief; user can release, send stage back or stop so subsequent run stages change; claim-check flags block until user chooses fix/publish/stop. - Boundary reachability: Supported locked up mode always runs the model Lead and includes user-facing stage/final gates and inbox when configured; the same installed engine routes them. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The Lead exercises model-backed discretion on whole-run briefs and intervention, not merely tool routing. Separate legitimate parent decisions control substantive stage/final acceptance, while role/parallel limits are enforcement support. - Evidence: packages/runtime/src/engine/project.js; packages/runtime/src/engine/broker.js; packages/node/src/policy/delegate.js; docs/design/runtime.md; README.md. - Basis: structural, normal installed runtime - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Role topology is fixed by the locked human-authored design; no arbitrary autonomous organization redesign is implied. A simple single-tool approval does not qualify as parent S3.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge worker outputs and Lead&#39;s source-attributed answer against direct work files and source cards before final acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overconfident or unsubstantiated research claims, wrong prices/dates, flawed worker reports and missing evidence despite a plausible summary. - Claim being audited: A role&#39;s submitted work supports the user decision and the Lead&#39;s tagged output claims are backed by cited sources. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker&#39;s own work file, source card/summary, Lead outputs/result.md with claim tags and the normal orchestration transcript. - Complementary access path: A separately instantiated reviewer with clean context reads actual worker files and source cards; after write-up another checker turn reads raw result claims and independent sources, while deterministic URL health checks support the same audit. - Independence boundary: Checker/reviewer has separate role/session and clean prompt context; raw source cards/work/result files rather than worker self-report; model provider can be same or different and model reliability is not assumed. - Who acts on findings: The engine parses checker verdicts, sends revise findings to the original target for rework, escalates exceeding rounds to human, and flags contradicted/unknown claims in the human inbox; fix/stop/publish choice changes the result path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Substantively decide whether target work/answer claims are accepted, need revision or must be escalated based on independently supplied source evidence. - Decision owner: Separate real model-backed reviewer/checker session; deterministic source URL checking, verdict parsing and flag thresholds only enforce and transport the judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewerOf, cleanContext, parseReviewBlock, verdictEvents, mergeLanes, shouldFlag, fixed rounds and human escalation path. - Closure path: Reviewer verdict returns to runtime; revises role brief and new attempt or raises human flag; checker statuses/contradictions feed Lead write-up repair or publish-with-warning decision. - Boundary reachability: Shipped standard mas-office up automatically opens reviewer role when configured and runs claim checkers during verify; stock example demonstrates this configured mode without user-built reviewer plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent model reviewer makes an evidence-dependent judgment; a URL-up/down check or static threshold alone would not establish S3. - Evidence: packages/runtime/src/engine/project.js; packages/node/src/verify/verdicts.js; packages/node/src/verify/claims.js; docs/design/runtime.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No dedicated first-party outside-and-then organizational intelligence/adaptation loop is shown for the installed research/decision team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Emerging external sources, markets and task facts are read during research, but future viability changes of the organization are not monitored as a separate function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Would choose prospective structural/capability adaptations from external change and feed them into current team operation; no actor with that right in the installed runtime identified. - Decision owner: No installed S4-specific owner; finder roles research user tasks and Lead responds to current work, not future team viability. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WebSearch/WebFetch role tools, source cards, source-health evaluator and team setup are task evidence/current controls. - Closure path: No ongoing outside signal → future organization option → accepted change in team capabilities/roles → return to S3 current work loop demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Open-web research for a product comparison or trip is S1 work; future adaptation requires a distinct prospective choice/return not shown. - Evidence: packages/runtime/src/engine/project.js; packages/node/src/verify/sources.js; docs/design/runtime.md; README.md. - Basis: bounded source absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate identity-policy issue resolution and returned ruling distinct from team design, tool rules or current stage decisions is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Questions of the team&#39;s enduring purpose/identity or conflict between current delivery and future adaptation could require S5; ordinary plan and permission requests are task-level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A triggered identity-level ultimate policy judgment binding later operation would be required; the observed signature/lock is an existing owner-selected configuration gate. - Decision owner: The human is legitimate owner of team setup and tool policies, but no shipped issue-triggered S5 parent authority transaction and binding changed ultimate mission is shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: structure.locked, signed team signature, tool vocabulary, delegation rules, bounded budgets and default-deny policies; these bind existing rights but are not decisive identity-level dispute resolution. - Closure path: Tampered/unlocked structures refuse execution until relocked, but that is configuration integrity rather than an identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate adjudication → returned change in governing direction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A static role constitution, policy file or human approve/deny gate alone cannot supply S5; the system&#39;s owner controls present tasks and permission envelopes, not a reconstructed ultimate-purpose return loop. - Evidence: packages/runtime/src/engine/project.js; packages/node/src/team/sign.js; packages/node/src/policy/delegate.js; docs/design/runtime.md; README.md. - Basis: bounded source absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c18c1f335aaec9783ae6d15180fd8ec3dda5b4cc/assessments/ma/mas-office.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c18c1f335aaec9783ae6d15180fd8ec3dda5b4cc\">c18c1f3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T17:38:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ai-timon/timon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Timon</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ai-timon/timon/tree/15794bf8db23cb620a40645f273c9b18d7a4b030\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 15794bf8db23cb620a40645f273c9b18d7a4b030\">15794bf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: project worker missions turn assigned programming, research, maintenance and review tasks into outputs in their working environments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository/task contents, errors, uncertain steps, input from other workers and user corrections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task actions, edits, next tool calls or a completed/blocked response within the role&#39;s constraints. - Decision owner: the configured model-backed coder/researcher/etc. actor launched by Timon&#39;s installed agent composition; external CLI engines supply model execution but do not independently define Timon&#39;s roles, task commitments or orchestration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role templates, backend launch, process fence, tool preauthorization, mission persistence, per-task worktrees and task status API. - Closure path: the role receives task context, executes through the CLI process, reports the outcome via timon task done/block and the dispatcher/wake loop returns that outcome to the orchestrator and subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: standard onboarding/materialization creates complete project agents; ordinary dispatcher and daemon process adapters launch those configured roles without a user implementing a new agent harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed role leaves task routing and enforcement but not the context-sensitive choice of edits, research or substantive review judgment; removing Timon leaves provider CLIs but not this installed team workflow. - Evidence: agent role prompts and seeding; dispatcher routes to role missions; mission/backend process engine; README installation and agent-team boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the configured team; limited to integrations where an external CLI has actually been installed and authenticated. - Caveats: this does not imply model-provider control or verified real-task performance; external CLI/model execution is a mandatory dependency of agent-owned task discretion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concrete interference between simultaneously active code work cells and between a code/review/merge chain and tasks that depend on its integrated output. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent writers sharing a Git base could overwrite/conflict; a dependent coder could run on a branch missing a predecessor&#39;s unmerged changes; multiple review tasks could evaluate conflicting iterations. - Distinct S1 units: separate dispatched coder tasks, review missions and merger missions, which work on distinct task identities and associated Git branches. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping base updates and dependency races between a completed code task and its outstanding review/merge; explicit source comments describe stale-base and duplicate-review failure modes. - Attenuating coordination relation: task-specific worktree/branch creation; dependency gates wait for review pass and merge completion; review-chain single-flight and iteration linkage; worktree base refresh with explicit conflict outcomes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a gated dependent remains waiting instead of executing on the wrong base; review change requests create a next coder iteration; base-refresh conflicts return an explicit path for the acting agent. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: worktree isolation, gated integration and conflicting-iteration control directly address identified concurrent-writing and stale-evidence disturbance, not just a message bus or queue. - Decisive decision or feedback right: set/revise the inter-work-cell isolation and dependency/merge coordination response, distinct from choosing each worker&#39;s task. - Decision owner: Timon&#39;s first-party developer-encoded coordination primitive in the shipped runtime. A model-backed agent does not demonstrably exercise autonomous discretion over revising the conflict-control policy across all concurrent cells. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worktree manager, commit/dependency metadata, merge gate, caps and event bus. - Closure path: dispatch checks branch/dependency state and requeues or admits a task; workers then operate only when the relevant integration state can support their next actions. - Boundary reachability: dispatcher imports createWorktree, evaluateDeps and refreshWorktreeBase in the shipped daemon; no third-party coordinator must be authored first. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordination relation is real and function-specific but fixed in source and enforced deterministically. Task/agent autonomy does not automatically transfer ownership of these S2 choices. - Evidence: worktree setup and refresh; merge/dependency integration gates; dispatcher and concurrency; code/review/merge chain deduplication. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: C is limited to this specifically evidenced source-native S2 path, not a claim that every cross-project team conflict has an autonomous negotiator.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: control current whole-series/project commitments by breaking work into role-assigned tasks, observing blockers/returns, choosing next interventions and distributing execution across models/roles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked or failing tasks, exhausted backends, stalled review chains, quota pressure and competing commitments over a finite worker pool. - Whole-system current view: project orchestrator obtains series/task state, completion recaps, blocked tasks and role/series plans; source explicitly seeds the orchestrator to follow rather than itself execute worker code. - Current-control decision scope: the model-backed project/main orchestrator can replan the active work, send diagnostic/recovery missions, abandon or escalate blocked series; in an operator-plan mode the user selects model/provider resources for the whole task series. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which current task/role needs attention and which commitment and resource allocation the project should pursue next. - Decision owner: in the base mode, the model-backed orchestrator whose mission receives synthesized returns; in the parent mode, the human selects the series plan&#39;s per-role/provider allocations and the running orchestrator applies those choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task database, admission caps, role routing, resource watchdog and fixed day/night quotas, backoff, wake-trigger scheduling, model-selection validation. - Closure path: worker completion/failure triggers recap, the orchestrator receives it and chooses a next task or escalation; the UI&#39;s human-selected series plan returns to the agent as commands, and the agent applies role/provider choices before the tasks are dispatched. - Boundary reachability: orchestrator seeding and wake/dispatcher are standard first-party runtime; the series-plan UI question and per-role configuration are shipped, not a possible future plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in the base mode, fixed resource caps do not select the substantive intervention; the agent does. In the distinct parent mode, the human&#39;s whole-series allocation judgment is the deciding authority, not merely a permission to execute a shell command. - Evidence: orchestrator role and its decisions; wake recaps and escalation; series-plan user choices and applied commands; series-wide role/provider enforcement; resource-only enforcement. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the parent mode is limited to the explicitly supported user-controlled series-wide provider/task allocation; a generic allow/deny card, service restart or fixed hardware cap is not counted as S3 governance. No live mission traces were executed.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge the coder&#39;s ordinary completed-task claim by independent examination of the actual changed Git state before integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an agent can report completion while its patch has defects, violates requirements or fails correctness/security review. - Claim being audited: the coder&#39;s code task and underlying branch are fit to merge. - Ordinary reporting path: coder&#39;s task closure and mission recap. - Complementary access path: separately seeded reviewer role directly reads branch diff/commit evidence, returns verdict: pass or verdict: changesrequested with actionable findings. - Independence boundary: reviewer has a different role prompt and task identity, is explicitly barred from editing, and uses underlying Git diff rather than only trusting coder&#39;s done comment. Separate model identity is configurable and need not imply independent model vendors. - Who acts on findings: verification-loop parses the reviewer judgment; a failed review creates a bounded new code iteration with feedback, and pass creates a merge task; invalid verdict blocks/escalates rather than silently integrating. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent substantive judgment of whether the actual patch passes or needs changes. - Decision owner: a separate model-backed reviewer actor in the ordinary configured code/review/merge pipeline; the parser and verification loop only enforce its verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: diff-stat preparation, review role tool restrictions, verdict parser, iteration cap, merge dependency and logs. - Closure path: reviewer outcome changesrequested feeds into the next coder iteration; a pass unlocks the merger; unparseable review blocks and notifies the supervising orchestrator. - Boundary reachability: handleCodeDone explicitly generates a review task and resolves/seeds a reviewer in the shipped full pipeline. It can be intentionally disabled in code-only mode, which is not credited as an S3 mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a bounded, read-only independent reviewer makes the quality judgment on complementary raw source evidence; deterministic parser/guard alone would not decide defect severity. The system&#39;s own patch audit is distinct from external security audit provided as a product task. - Evidence: reviewer identity, read-only rules and verdict contract; code-to-review-to-repair/merge closure; verdict parser; dependency closure gate. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: no empirical independence or recall measurement; a reviewer can share a provider family with the coder. Code-only and no-diff paths do not provide this audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: produce prospective project-improvement and external-opportunity options relative to the owner&#39;s stated longer-term objectives. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing outside market/tool opportunities, future project requirements and mismatch between existing portfolio and objectives. - External distinction: the bundled opportunity phase explicitly uses WebSearch/WebFetch for external trends, demand and tools; improvement phase combines current project scores, prior failures and accepted objectives. - Future / prospective distinction: nightly dreaming generates options, cost/conviction rationales and first steps for potential later work, rather than just responding to an immediate operational task. - Adaptation option generated: an OSS-shipped dreamproposals queue contains prospective suggestions, source links and human review actions. - Path back into current capability / S3: an operator can accept/reject/snooze the specific proposed option in the bundled Dreams UI; acceptance is recorded, but the inspected code does not itself create an executing Kanban task, reconfigure the agent&#39;s current capability or automatically integrate source. That subsequent implementation/commitment loop requires a human/developer composition. - Decisive decision or feedback right: assess future environment signals and select action-worthy options for the project portfolio. - Decision owner: shipped dedicated agent prompt generates the options, but final prioritization/translation of accepted proposals into present controlled commitments is left to an operator. This establishes a function-specific S4 constructor path rather than an autonomously closed S4 adaptation loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: nightly timer, raw project score, deduplication, proposal database, notification, web tools, human status route. - Closure path: prospective proposals reach a structured review queue; the absence of automatic activation into present work limits the state to C, not A or P. - Boundary reachability: startDreamer ships in the daemon and the Dreams route/UI includes accepting/rejecting proposals. A configured supported provider with WebSearch/WebFetch is needed for live external research. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses future options but source does not show it owning a complete adaptation decision that changes current capability; a generic browser or memory file alone would not be enough, whereas this future-oriented, external-evidence-to-proposal path is explicit. - Evidence: nightly prospective web research, proposal insert; human accept/reject route; Dreams UI decisions; project priority signals. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: C does not grant automatic or closed autonomous adaptation; no evidence that accepting a dream automatically creates a resource-backed task. The separate internal memory-curation routine alone is not credited as external S4.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: possible owner-level identity/ultimate-policy guidance for a local cross-project agent team; a policy-issue decision and authoritative returned resolution are not decisively reconstructed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing project priorities and future directions can be reconciled against user-set objectives, but the boundary of an actual identity-policy issue versus normal task preference is unclear. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the team&#39;s ultimate direction in the face of strategic conflicts, not simply authorize an individual tool or choose a model. - Decision owner: the human user edits an objectives document and controls the acceptance of dream proposals; source establishes a possible parent direction, but not a bounded, issue-triggered S5 resolution transaction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: objectives.md, cross-project digest, project scorer, dream directives and approvals. - Closure path: the objectives document feeds the globalcontext agent&#39;s mission digest and scorer; however, the connection from an explicit identity-level issue or S3–S4 conflict to a resolved ultimate-policy choice and its enforced return is not established by the inspected material. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither routine tool approval nor editable objectives automatically establish S5; equally, the shipped objectives-to-cross-project-context path is concrete enough that this review does not assert a bounded absence without additional source/operating-mode evidence. - Evidence: objectives digest; conditional digest injection; objective-based scoring; dream directives and proposals; dream review route. - Basis: structural and unknown. - Confidence: low for a positive S5 claim; high for the described first-party signal paths. - Caveats: ? is specific to the potentially in-boundary objectives/policy loop and its unproven identity-level decision/closure, not a default negative state. OSS repo maintainer policy is an excluded higher recursion.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/91abaa6bf94a5fbb962e3c89fafe5acfcdd11707/assessments/ti/timon.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 91abaa6bf94a5fbb962e3c89fafe5acfcdd11707\">91abaa6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:05:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skrvidz/agentic-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agentic Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skrvidz/agentic-harness/tree/c67b6cec088d8f0054d9626adad6a22ea87fdbd6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c67b6cec088d8f0054d9626adad6a22ea87fdbd6\">c67b6ce &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-led project implementation via Claude or Codex engineer turns, including tool/repository feedback and task revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing local source and tests, uncertain implementation requirements, reviewer defects, failed commands and subsequent model observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose code edits, test/inspection commands, interpretation of feedback and implementation strategy inside the supplied objective/current task. - Decision owner: Spawned engineer model CLI as explicit task-operating actor, bounded by Harness and its model/tool permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ClaudeRunner/CodexEngineerRunner, persistent state, per-run worktrees, test evidence, max-cycles and reviewer feedback file. - Closure path: Human objective → engineer model turn and code edits → terminal/tool feedback → engine captures outcome → separate review → revised task fed into next engineer turn or final result. - Boundary reachability: agent-harness run and workspace Run create a real engineer subprocess, while --dry-run is explicitly separate and not credited for autonomous operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Engineer model chooses substantive implementation; removing it leaves only runner/orchestration and scripted test fixtures. - Evidence: CLI invocation, subprocess runners, engineer turn. - Basis: structural first-party executable path. - Confidence: High for decision-loop wiring, medium for actual provider behavior without an authenticated run. - Caveats: The harness does not implement provider transport; installed authenticated CLI models are required outside dry-run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No qualifying S2 loop that detects operational-unit interaction-induced disturbance and returns attenuation into independent peer behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel plan tickets may conflict during Git integration, or dependencies may block scheduling; these are prevented/flagged by fixed isolation/dependency checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Static scheduler selects ready DAG nodes; merge conflicts fail/pause rather than a distinct S2 coordinator negotiating or adapting peer commitments. - Decision owner: Model planner authored the initial task graph; deterministic PlanHarness enforces it with fixed width, worktrees and merge checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Validated dependson, ready[:width], bounded asyncio.gather, isolated TicketWorkspace and fail-on-conflict integration. - Closure path: Plan → independent ticket loops → deterministic merge or pause; no observed conflict signal → inter-S1 attenuation decision → peer behavior correction path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A dependency graph and isolation are generic task sequencing. Model planning is not proven to monitor competing peers and damp induced interference during current execution. - Evidence: DAG scheduler, worktrees/merge. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Model could have chosen safer dependencies ex ante, but a hypothetical conflict-attenuation behavior is not an evidenced first-party closed S2 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Current-goal control of implementation commitments and task direction based on whole-run state and independent evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Code that does not satisfy goal/acceptance tests, unresolved scope decisions, an incomplete increment, and uncertainty whether a wider ticket plan achieved the objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer decides APPROVED with a next task, REVISE with required changes, DONE or NEEDSHUMAN; the plan-level evaluator judges the integrated project against the whole original objective and may pause delivery. - Decision owner: Dedicated reviewer model for the single-run organization; in plan mode, dedicated planner/final-evaluator model for whole-ticket commitment judgment. Harness applies decisions to future task/current status. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed review schema, recorded run state/criteria, feedback file, nextactor transition, bounded cycle budget, SQLite event artifacts and final-plan evaluation. - Closure path: Current project evidence + engineer work → reviewer compares objective/criteria → chooses revision/next increment/completion/human gate → engine adjusts the next S1 task or stops. In DAG mode, integrated ticket results and source evidence → planner&#39;s final verdict → whole-goal completion or pause. - Whole-system current view: reviewerprompt receives the complete current objective/criteria and source-backed diff/status/test evidence; plannerfinalprompt additionally receives every ticket outcome, full plan and integrated-repo evidence. - Current-control decision scope: Approve a sound increment and redirect the next task, require corrective rework, declare current run complete or require human decision; final plan acceptance can be withheld. This is discretionary present-task commitment control, not a deterministic max-cycle or width guard. - Boundary reachability: Every default engineer turn is followed by reviewer control in Harness.runasync; plan mode invokes final model evaluation after all ticket integrations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects the quality/commitment verdict and next operational instruction from evidence; the Python engine merely branches on the verdict to update S1 commitments. Parent human involvement is a pause/answer gate, not claimed as a separate S3(P) mode. - Evidence: review verdict and actions, current-state prompts, final planner control. - Basis: explicit + structural first-party model choice and return path. - Confidence: Medium-high; model verdict rights are explicit though semantic quality is unobserved in live providers. - Caveats: No independent fleet budget arbitration claimed; scoped to goal commitment/current work in one configured harness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate evidence-facing review of operational completion claims and test outcomes before continued implementation or acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Engineer can confidently report a completed change that fails acceptance criteria, hides untracked files or contains regressions not captured in its summary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Read-only reviewer supplies structured findings and REVISE/DONE/APPROVED verdict from direct repository/test evidence; engine records it and routes corrections. - Decision owner: Distinct Codex reviewer CLI session (fresh read-only sandbox), separate from edit-capable engineer; the independent final planner evaluates integrated evidence in DAG mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: collectevidence runs real Git diff/status/untracked listing and optional project test command; review prompt calls engineer text untrusted; Codex --sandbox read-only and JSON schema; persisted audit raw artifacts. - Closure path: Engineer&#39;s report → independent evidence capture plus reviewer primary-source inspection → verdict and cited findings → reviewerfeedback.md/currenttask → next engineer turn, or blocked human decision/completion. - Claim being audited: Work delivered in the active turn meets the stated objective and acceptance criteria and can be accepted as done or as a sound increment. - Ordinary reporting path: The engineer summary and external CLI stream, persisted as pendingengineersummary and transcript artifacts. - Complementary access path: Git diff/stat/status and untracked file listing from the actual working tree, configured test-command output and reviewer direct read-only access to the code, plus independent integrated working-tree evidence for final plan review. - Independence boundary: Separate subprocess agent and session; fresh Codex reviewers start under read-only sandbox, cannot directly modify engineer&#39;s work. Captured evidence is provided by harness code, not inferred from engineer text; resumed sessions retain prior policy. - Who acts on findings: Harness.applyreview converts REVISE into the next engineer assignment; NEEDSHUMAN forces a human question; DONE gates completion. Planner can withhold whole-plan completion. - Boundary reachability: Review is mandatory after each successful engineer turn in default Harness, with nextactor=reviewer durable even when the cycle limit is exhausted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent evidence-backed reviewer judgments are not the same as generic logs or unit tests; they determine whether the operation is revised, continued or completed. - Evidence: evidence collection, read-only reviewer runner, review feedback return. - Basis: explicit + structural independent reviewer path. - Confidence: High for separation, evidence path and corrective wiring; no provider-backed audit of actual generated changes performed here. - Caveats: Missing configured test command is marked unverified; evidence bounded/truncated to 40k chars, and a read-only CLI sandbox still relies on vendor client enforcement outside the first-party product.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective organizational adaptation that changes future harness capabilities in response to an external environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future implementation milestones are decomposed from the present task; no separate environmental foresight is continuously collected to change agent organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model planner defines the current goal&#39;s ticket list, but cannot alter the harness&#39;s future capability, governance or strategy; user changes model/settings manually. - Decision owner: Planner for near-term task plan and human for future system configuration; no S4 actor established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DAG plan/review, stored transcripts and model selection configuration. - Closure path: Goal → model-authored near-term tickets → implementation; no future environment scan → adaptation option → selection → return to future operating capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Time-ordered ticket planning is present operational decomposition, not strategic outside-and-then environmental intelligence. - Evidence: planner prompt, DAG execution. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An outside organization could adopt findings to redesign its tooling, but that return is not wired in the assessed package.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No recurring ultimate organizational identity and policy arbitration loop within the product. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe edits, unsupported decisions and risky plan changes are bounded by configured CLI sandbox/limits and human pause. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human sets objective and decides blocked product/scope questions; no first-party actor adjudicates enduring identity or constitutional policy. - Decision owner: Human user for objective and particular decision; editor/CLI sandbox for fixed access bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Human decision callback/CLI, max cycles, explicit noninteractive pause, read-only reviewer setting and worktree constraints. - Closure path: Risk/product question → human decision → next engineer task, or configured hard limit → pause; neither constitutes policy identity dispute → ultimate arbitration → binding organizational-policy change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Operational question/approval and fixed sandbox policy are not S5 policy-authority decision rights. - Evidence: human decision handling, README safety boundary. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: User&#39;s employer or external host may retain ultimate policy authority outside this local harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/717a8b4606aa4ea034e6108cf2200e696f90de56/assessments/ag/agentic-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 717a8b4606aa4ea034e6108cf2200e696f90de56\">717a8b4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:25:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Sinkear/agentwork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agentwork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Sinkear/agentwork/tree/3aecbef55ac694d64cb77403092aa850279b60a8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3aecbef55ac694d64cb77403092aa850279b60a8\">3aecbef &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: CLI-model execution of assigned goals and delegated sub-goal code/work tasks, with context and tool-result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing repo contents, requirements, test failures, independent reviewer verdicts, integration-base shifts, consulted advice and human rejection reasons. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose substantive work/edits/tool calls and responses in owner/sub-goal runs; local assignee chooses how to repair a rejected or conflicting revision. - Decision owner: External connected CLI model running under first-party agentwork role/identity token as an explicit operational actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Daemon/machine dispatcher, per-run worktree, assigned agent persona, ACP/JSONL/JSON-RPC transport, role-scoped CLI, timeouts/watchdogs, stateful resume and Git worktree snapshot. - Closure path: Human goal → owner run/model plans/directs → assignee model executes local project changes and reports → run terminal/verification signals → feedback/wake/context into successor agent turns → revised task or completion. - Boundary reachability: Documented agentwork connect launches configured agent CLI runtimes on machine dispatch, and the first-party service creates owner/sub-goal runs with explicit role context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The models decide which sub-goals to create, tools to invoke and code changes to make; deterministic state reconciliation and permission checks are support, not task reasoning. - Evidence: README quick start, run prompt assembly, sub-goal creation. - Basis: explicit + structural first-party operational integration. - Confidence: High for implemented role/feedback reachability, medium for live external-model behavior. - Caveats: No authenticated target-domain agent process was launched in this review; internal behaviors of ACP/CLI backends are not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Detect and attenuate conflicts between independently produced sub-goal changes, directing the responsible assignee to rebase/rework rather than silently integrating an incompatible revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different S1 sub-goal units edit against a shared goal branch and create incompatible integration bases or merged changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A failed owner integration marks a specific Change conflicting, invalidates its prior verified state and enqueues the same assignee on the latest goal base; owner later chooses integration of a new ready revision. - Decision owner: First-party constructor-owned service transition and event/goal coordinator establish the S2-specific return, with model owner initiating integration and assignee choosing the concrete repair. No independent agent-owned S2-specific attenuation policy is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-run isolated branches/worktrees, Change and ordered revisions, integratechange, conditional transitions, qualityiteration and idempotent run enqueue. - Closure path: Parallel sub-goal outputs → owner integrates one into shared branch → subsequent integration conflict → service marks conflict and enqueues affected assignee → assignee reworks against newer base → new revision becomes ready → owner retries integration. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent independently assigned sub-goal implementation runs, each with its own assignee/worktree and Change against the same goal branch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A previous sub-goal&#39;s integration changes the shared base, making another independently completed change incompatible at merge. - Attenuating coordination relation: Conflict-specific MarkChangeIntegrated(false) returns the disrupted sub-goal to rework instead of accepting stale output; Change revision history preserves the old failure/new base relation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The assignee receives a successor run and prompt text explicitly identifying the prior conflicting Change and requirement to resolve it on the new integration base. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: A concrete disturbance is generated between independently produced S1 changes, then causes a targeted adjustment in the affected peer&#39;s subsequent work; static worktree isolation alone is not the positive witness. - Boundary reachability: The documented sub-goal/change-integration workflow invokes MarkChangeIntegrated on failed Git integration, with the recovery path wired into normal owner/assignee runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The relation is first-party fixed conflict policy (C), while agent workers provide the actual revision and owner chooses when/what to merge; no general independent S2 judgment actor is shown. - Evidence: collaboration conflict contract, conflict transition/rework, assignee conflict wake. - Basis: explicit + structural constructor control with subsequent operational return. - Confidence: High for specific first-party conflict-loop mechanics. - Caveats: C does not assert a free-standing model coordinator that negotiates all possible peer conflicts; ordinary consult/handoff alone are not counted as S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Own and regulate goal-wide current commitments — sub-goal assignment, integration/recovery and handover — with a separate legitimate human intervention mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing incomplete sub-goals, failed verifications, ready/conflicting changes, stalled owner runs, disputed acceptance and human-requested rework. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent owner may choose assignees/sub-goals, request help, transfer ownership and integrate ready changes from a current goal-wide state; parent human may redirect/assign and reject a goal in review, returning binding correction to the owner. - Decision owner: Agent/squad leader owner model in autonomous mode (A) under goal-role authority; human operator acting through review/assignment UI/API in parent mode (P). Coordinator derives attention but does not make these substantive decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal/sub-goal/change/verification SQLite state, role tokens and requireOwnerOf, owner-only CLI, event-driven OwnerAttention, guarded single owner run, human review state and feedback comments. - Closure path: Goal/sub-goal/change status → derived owner attention and current context → owner model allocates or integrates operational commitments → assignee execution and results → persisted state and next owner wake. Parent mode: goal-wide review evidence → human approve/reject/redirect → binding review transition → a rejected goal creates a new owner run with explicit human reason, or approval triggers delivery. - Whole-system current view: The owner can list goal/sub-goals/changes/verification and sees an actionable wake summary; human UI exposes goal progress, review and changed artifacts. This is the whole declared goal, not an unverified cross-domain fleet command center. - Current-control decision scope: Assigning work to specific S1 units, integrating/reworking their results, changing owner accountability, deciding continuation vs accepted delivery and returning human rejection priorities into next operation; distinct from static concurrency caps. - Boundary reachability: README and Collaboration.v2.md prescribe owner-only subgoal/integration/handoff tools; service rejects non-owner use, wakes owner after actionable changes and records human review decisions via ResolveReview. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The connected owner model owns substantive task division and integration decisions in agent-assigned mode. The human&#39;s accepted/rejected review and reassignment are distinct authorized parent interventions; the daemon only enforces transitions.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separately assigned agent verifier checks a sub-goal&#39;s actual output from an independent stable snapshot and can reject work before owner integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An assignee reports completed while produced code/result violates the sub-goal&#39;s declared requirements or quality expectations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Named verifier agent issues structured passed or rejected with evidence through verifysubgoal; rejected verdict prevents Change admission and enqueues another assignee run. - Decision owner: Explicitly configured distinct verifierid model acting in role=verify; platform authenticates verdict to that named verifier and owns deterministic state transition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Detached Git snapshot for verifier, separate verify run identity, no owner role authority, persisted verificationresult, agent-specific verdict tool and assignee rework loop. - Closure path: Assignee completes and source revision is frozen → verification run inspects stable snapshot → verifier model returns verdict/evidence → platform records result → rejected reopens task and enqueues the assignee with rejection summary, passed creates a ready Change for owner. - Claim being audited: The assignee&#39;s produced sub-goal deliverable is complete/valid enough to become an integrable Change. - Ordinary reporting path: Assignee&#39;s run completion, summary, commits and domain machine-check output recorded on the goal and sub-goal. - Complementary access path: Dedicated verifier run in a detached snapshot of the submitted sub-goal branch can inspect real work/files and issue independent verdict/evidence; named verifier&#39;s verifysubgoal call, not assignee self-report, closes the audit. - Independence boundary: Distinct configured verifier agent/worktree/run/role versus assignee, with a stable detached submitted ref. Service verifies verifier identity, status and running verdict window; a single-actor configuration or default machine verification does not demonstrate independent agent audit. The code validates the named verifierid but does not categorically forbid configuring it equal to assignee ID, so independence must be configured. - Who acts on findings: Service immediately transitions rejected sub-goals into new assignee work with qualityiteration increment and rejection summary injected; passed work goes to owner integration. Squad reviewer comments are separately human-advisory and are not the source of this positive claim. - Boundary reachability: The subgoal API supports verifierid, daemon dispatches role=verify on a stable source snapshot and VerifySubGoal commits the verdict plus subsequent correction run. This is a first-party standard optional mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model verifier decides the substantive completion judgment on independent source evidence; routine machine verification/guardrails are supporting checks only. - Evidence: sub-goal creation/verifier ID, verdict authority and rework, detached verifier workspace. - Basis: explicit + structural complementary audit mode. - Confidence: High for first-party configured verifier control, medium for actual independence when an operator does not select distinct agents. - Caveats: Default verifier is machine-command-based and not a positive agent S3 by itself; human review of squad opinions remains the human&#39;s verdict. Role-level sandbox relies on first-party worktree/role implementation and external execution environments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No established agent-owned or parent-closed prospective adaptation of the domain/goal organization&#39;s future capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tracker issues, schedules, acceptance failures and historical task results affect current/recurring work, not a decision on future organization design. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human defines domain policy and runtime/agent configuration; a processor can compile natural-language acceptance rules, but has no authority to choose prospective strategic changes in a future environmental model. - Decision owner: Operator for domain acceptance/agent roster; active agents for present work only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy-text compilation to deterministic checks, history, gate statistics, cron/issue triggers and domain reporting. - Closure path: Human policy → checks/gates → operational verification/delivery; schedule → clone current goal. Neither is environmental futures → adaptation options → strategic selection → reconfiguration return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Acceptance-policy compilation and recurring triggers are present control configuration, not prospective intelligence about external future requirements. - Evidence: policy and trigger overview, domain policy configuration. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A human may use historical results to redesign future capabilities outside this bounded first-party loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No declared closed ultimate-purpose/identity arbitration authority beyond human domain acceptance and operational gates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unapproved side effects, assignee role violation, poor task quality and agent handoff loops are bounded by service/human gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human may define domain acceptance, approve/reject a goal or stop a handoff loop; those are goal acceptance/operational continuation decisions rather than enduring identity/purpose arbitration. - Decision owner: Single human operator for policy configuration and review actions; deterministic service for permission checks. No recurring S5 issue adjudicator distinct from S3 control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal ownership authority, role tokens, acceptance-policy freeze, human checkpoint, handoff-loop guard and audit logs. - Closure path: Human config → operational acceptance checks/permissions, or review → goal rejection/approval → next run/delivery; no mission/identity conflict → legitimate ultimate-policy judgment → changed binding constitution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Human final approval does not automatically imply VSM S5; even a domain policy string is an execution constraint rather than an ultimate organizational identity dispute loop. - Evidence: domain/goal policy, review resolution, role authority. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Ultimate identity/purpose decisions may exist in the real operator&#39;s institution, outside this open-source single-user software boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1bce7f1a2ff3c599516a40edbd064de106784076/assessments/ag/agentwork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1bce7f1a2ff3c599516a40edbd064de106784076\">1bce7f1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T12:59:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orchestragent/orchestragent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">orch</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orchestragent/orchestragent/tree/533d06b440654e0f9c9654c80945a751796747e5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 533d06b440654e0f9c9654c80945a751796747e5\">533d06b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Generate and repair code in response to scoped user specification and feedback from tests and model calls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar source, incomplete interfaces, implementation errors, rejected model outputs and failing frozen tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a task solution/patch from specification, context and feedback, including changed implementation on failure. - Decision owner: Model-backed planner and builder calls in the installed run; deterministic code enforces schema, attempts and test acceptance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worktrees, contextpack, prompt budgets, frozen tests, provider adaptors, retry ladder. - Closure path: Model plan → task/test contracts → model patch → sandbox test → accept or issue failure feedback → reattempt/replan/park. - Boundary reachability: plan and execute are first-party orchestrator operations reachable through supported CLI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The planner and code model choose substantive task decomposition and edits; the scheduler does not author those solutions. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L715-L885, orchrun.py#L3247-L3477, orchrun.py#L3848-L3905. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for a reachable source-level loop; no provider-run success reproduced. - Caveats: The normal run requires configured model inference and an executable project environment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: Parallel independently model-backed task builders, each operating in its own task-specific worktree against the same shared integration branch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A builder&#39;s code or contract can overlap another builder&#39;s changed files, produce a Git conflict, or invalidate a frozen test already passed by another task. - Attenuating coordination relation: Planned file ownership/dependencies, serialized merge lock, affected-tests integration gate and explicit capped rebasing of collided tasks. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Conflict or affected-test failure returns the named task to READY/rebase or retry with updated branch state rather than silently accepting both edits. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The implemented merge-collision-to-task-retry and cross-task test-interference gate regulate actual interaction failures between distinct coding units. - Function: Attenuate interference between parallel code-building units in a shared integration branch. - Disturbance / variety regulated: File ownership overlap, incompatible dependency interfaces, parallel Git conflicts and tests broken by another worker&#39;s merge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Coordinate dependency/ownership and merge order, reject a conflicting commit and route the affected task back for a refreshed build. - Decision owner: Deterministic DAG scheduler, interface and file gates and integration merge lock; no separate autonomous coordination actor owns the arbitration policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planned dependency edges, serialized integration, affected-test gate, rebase count and changed-worktree tests. - Closure path: Concurrent task outputs → merge collision/affected tests → reject/rebase/retry of specific task → next task execution on updated integration state. - Boundary reachability: execute, ready, merge are standard paths for concurrent task runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The collision/feedback witness is function-specific and shipped, but the coordinator&#39;s decisive scheduling/merge arbitration is hard-coded; agent-owned S2 would need composition. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L2170-L2257, orchrun.py#L2420-L2475, orchrun.py#L3848-L3910. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Mere DAG edges would not establish S2 without the identified collision-response/return path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Whole-system current view: The run&#39;s persisted task table, DONE/READY/RUNNING/PARKED statuses, worker occupancy, rate/spend counters and outstanding dependency graph. - Current-control decision scope: Adjust current concurrency and admissions, reorder ready work by dependency criticality, or stop a module with repeated parked tasks; all are currently code-controlled rather than model-owned decisions. - Function: Control the complete current spec-run portfolio&#39;s admissions, throughput, resource limits and stalled tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider rate-limit pressure, too many active model calls, spend caps, blocked dependencies and repeating module failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Change current concurrency, dispatch priority and task admissions or park a failed module based on observed whole-run state. - Decision owner: Deterministic scheduler (drain, room, throttle, breaker); models generate tasks but do not autonomously decide whole-system resource intervention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AIMD limit, token/cache policies, dependency depth sorting, persistent state, failure counts. - Closure path: Ready/in-flight state and failure signals → scheduler resource/priority intervention → changed dispatch and retry conditions for multiple tasks. - Boundary reachability: Standard execution uses drain and applies current maxjobs/maxverifyjobs, rate and spend gates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A whole-run feedback and resource-decision path exists, but its discretionary control policy is fixed in code, leaving agent ownership uncomposed. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L2170-L2294, orchrun.py#L2451-L2490. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium; no autonomous allocator or qualified human parent mode evidenced. - Caveats: Ordinary user-set quotas do not independently establish P parent governance.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: The planner&#39;s frozen test contract correctly expresses the original user requirements, notwithstanding a builder&#39;s failing implementation or allegation of wrong tests. - Ordinary reporting path: The builder&#39;s candidate code and sandbox failure result are reported to the retry ladder and orchestration state. - Complementary access path: A separately prompted Claude reviewer receives original specification, requirement mapping, frozen test sources and explicit dispute, not just the builder&#39;s success report. - Independence boundary: Separate persistent review ID, pinned input hash and validated testscorrect/testswrong verdict; the builder cannot directly rewrite frozen tests. - Who acts on findings: review closes a correct-test dispute or fixtests commits guarded test corrections and re-verifies affected candidate and prior merges. - Function: Independently challenge the frozen test contract against the original user specification when task failures indicate a test dispute. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Wrong or over-constraining tests drafted before implementation, misleading failure attribution and builder attempts to relax constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An exception-review model independently returns testscorrect or testswrong, with an explanation and guarded test edits; a wrong-test verdict can authorize corrective test revision. - Decision owner: Separate Claude review invocation using original specification, protected from the builder&#39;s own test-edit decision; deterministic code validates the verdict and safe application. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable review inputs, durable review store, assertion-count no-weakening gate and regression check of already merged tasks. - Closure path: Failed worker/test dispute → independent review → validated finding → corrected frozen tests or rejection → saved candidate reverified / task retried. - Boundary reachability: review and fixtests are first-party retry-ladder operations; their execution is conditional, not an asserted always-on audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer has the substantive adjudication right; the builder cannot directly alter the pre-frozen test contract, while code checks and transports the verdict. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L3010-L3140, orchreview.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the dispute-triggered complementary path. - Caveats: Independence is functional/prompt-and-role separation, not a demonstrated separate provider organization; ordinary final pytest gates alone would not establish A.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - External distinction: Past external project specifications, sandbox/test outcomes and model-builder failures vary between runs; rule candidates are grounded in these encountered environmental distinctions, not only an internal heartbeat. - Future / prospective distinction: The A/B and replay checks compare candidate rules against distinct past specifications to select instructions expected to improve subsequent planning/building runs. - Adaptation option generated: Model-authored, cross-task reusable planner or builder lessons become candidate rules, tested with/without them and potentially activated at later checkpoints. - Path back into current capability / S3: Promoted rules enter the planner or builder prompt layer in a subsequent run, changing the model&#39;s future action repertoire, while harmful rules are retired. - Function: Learn prospective planning/building-rule changes from accumulated cross-run failures and outcomes, and return successful changes into future model prompts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeating planning defects, weak tests, recurring builder mistakes and the possibility that a proxy for eventual success becomes misleading. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model review/replanning/build attempts formulate generalizable lessons; A/B evidence evaluates whether a candidate helps, with checkpoint promotion and subsequent prompt influence. - Decision owner: Autonomous models propose the adaptive rule content from task observations; deterministic promotion statistics retain activation veto, so the agent does not own final promotion discretion in isolation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rule store, paired trials and replay, proxy-vs-real Spearman trust test, lifecycle candidate → active → retired and prompt layer budget. - Closure path: Outcomes and model lessons from earlier runs → candidate rule → with/without evaluation → promote/retire at checkpoint → altered next-plan/builder instruction. - Boundary reachability: learn, evolve, settleplannerrules and builder rule trials are first-party modes (automatic rule learning disabled in frozen-policy modes). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Autonomous models originate strategic adaptation content, not merely execute a preselected fixed retry; promotion is a statistical safeguard rather than a model-authored option. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L1570-L1775, orchrun.py#L3888-L3925, orchrules.py#L1-L125. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium for prospectively reusable operating-policy learning; causal effectiveness not independently reproduced. - Caveats: Feedback comes chiefly from past run/task outcomes, not market sensing. If an evaluator requires a wholly independent outside-environment sensor rather than cross-run experience, S4 should be revisited; the observed loop is durable future capability change, not just current repair.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-system identity or ultimate-policy dispute and legitimate-authority closure is evidenced for a spec-run organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe workspace edits, mismatched model routes, insufficient budgets and altered test contracts are addressed as operational guardrails. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Bind an ultimate policy/identity judgment to later operations; no such accountable agent or legitimate parent governance loop is shipped. - Decision owner: User chooses configuration and optional frozen policy; model reviews resolve tests and task strategy, not organizational identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy store, sandbox boundary, explicit model settings, pinned planning inputs, cancel/park limits and review gate. - Closure path: Configuration and safety gates constrain a run; no S5-level issue/appeal → authoritative decision → returned binding identity policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Guarded model choices and an immutable execution policy are not themselves an S5 constitutional decision. - Evidence: orchpolicy.py, orchrun.py#L715-L815. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer policy and a human&#39;s optional local run selection are outside this selected recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/987160f64eccd59b120a491c9f00becefacad4b3/assessments/or/orchestragent-orch.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 987160f64eccd59b120a491c9f00becefacad4b3\">987160f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:25:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mimshins/ork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mimshins/ork/tree/e97d75c554095ff41d79ef91fafbbdc8bcfc8b64\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e97d75c554095ff41d79ef91fafbbdc8bcfc8b64\">e97d75c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Claude sessions in a workflow transform requests into code, specifications, reviews and other local project outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user requests, repository state, test failures, tool results and child reports within each session&#39;s bounded task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete implementation/review actions and revise work from the tool, branch and report feedback. - Decision owner: model-driven Claude agent node in the first-party runtime; an operator supplies the initial task and permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph activation, SDK/CLI driver, tool allowlists, worktrees, event store, session budget and schema validation. - Closure path: agent chooses tools and edits, receives tool/result feedback, changes work and returns a structured report to its graph/leader. - Boundary reachability: claude.worker/claude.leader nodes and their tool/report bridge are supported installed node types, exercised structurally by shipped example workflows; no third-party agent&#39;s hidden control architecture is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: actions in a live session remain discretionary even though workflow graph edges, budgets and output transport are deterministic. - Evidence: scheduler built-ins and delegation, delegation port, factory example. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider execution was not performed in this review; operator credentials and the chosen workflow are prerequisites.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate competing write access among parallel S1 sessions in the same run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two concurrent writing agents can attempt overlapping files/folders or shared named resources, introducing overwrites or merge conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agents select resources to claim, release them and respond to a refused claim by choosing another task or negotiating with the holder; the registry deterministically detects overlap. - Decision owner: agent session for claim selection and the corrective work choice; deterministic claim registry for overlap verdict, holder identity, event delivery and automatic release. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: advisory claim registry keyed by normalized paths, refusal and release notices, worktree isolation and message channels. - Closure path: a conflicting claim is refused with holder identity; the agent receives that response, can communicate/replan, and may retry after a release notification, changing the subsequent S1 work target. - Boundary reachability: claim/release tools are supplied to writing Claude nodes when a run exposes parallel writers; the scheduler binds the run-scoped claim port. This is a shipped operating mode, not a hypothetical custom integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ork does not choose the agent&#39;s alternative work in response to contention; its registry only determines the fixed overlap rule. Claim discipline is advisory, so not every actual edit is protected. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently running writer leader/worker nodes or spawned worker copies with separate tasks and checkouts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping resource/path edits and resultant destructive interference, specifically documented as the reason for claims. - Attenuating coordination relation: explicit per-resource claim refusal, release, holder/contender notification and optional peer messaging. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the refused agent sees the holding node and can work elsewhere or ask it; release notice tells the agent to claim again before editing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: resource overlap is checked in executable first-party code, with a conflict-specific refusal/release feedback loop; message passing is only a support channel. - Evidence: claim registry implementation, Claude claim tool, claims and message specification. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: autonomous conflict handling is agent-instructed but not hard-enforced and was not exercised with a provider here; if the claimed function is defined solely as lock acquisition, that deterministic subdecision is not agent-owned.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: control a multi-child run&#39;s current commitments and exceptions at a configured leader/whole-run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child failure, run budget/concurrency saturation, exhausted revision loops, and conflicting current delivery commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the leader can choose whether and where to delegate/retry in light of child reports; an owner can answer an exhausted-loop exception, while configuration author sets the initial hard limits. - Decision owner: first-party leader/owner decision ports are available; workflow author must compose a genuinely whole-run supervisory objective and authority envelope. The deterministic runner owns enforcement, not the resource-policy judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: slot/budget accounting, maxWorkers cap, statuses, reports, loop counter, human fallback and halt semantics. - Closure path: delegated children return outcomes and costs; the leader can select follow-on work, or answer an exhausted loop to stop/extend/go onward; the scheduler executes the selected transition. - Boundary reachability: leader tools, child reports and owner-question/answer loop are shipped in the scheduler and Claude runtime; their S3-specific whole-run composition depends on the declared workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these are real current-intervention primitives, but a generic leader&#39;s task division alone does not establish a default autonomous whole-run S3 owner for arbitrary user workflows. This supports the narrower constructor C, not universal A. - Whole-system current view: available child reports, run status, costs and configured limits; whether they form an actionable whole-system view is workflow-dependent. - Current-control decision scope: child commitments and retry/loop continuation under configured budgets; no assertion that the agent can revise operator-specified global policy. - Evidence: leader/child delegation, loop owner decision, scheduler budget. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no parent-mode suffix from ordinary human gates, pause/stop or local approval; no runtime end-to-end current-control trace tested.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: inspect implementation claims through separately tasked agents that can directly read the produced branch rather than rely only on the builder&#39;s report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: defects in the builder&#39;s asserted completion, including security, correctness, UX and code-quality issues overlooked by ordinary production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent readonly reviewer sessions judge the produced work and return pass/changesrequested; the builder then receives review status and revises in a bounded loop. - Decision owner: separate reviewer Claude agents in the first-party software-factory opt-in mode; graph routing enforces returned verdicts, but is not the audit judge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: readonly checkout, fresh reviewer sessions, join statuses, bounded edge maxRounds, agent approver and optional human publication gate. - Closure path: builder branch is inspected by separately running security/UX/code reviewers; a changesrequested result routes back to builder and triggers repair; passing reviews route to the approver. - Boundary reachability: examples/software-factory.ork.yaml is a distributed executable Ork workflow invoking the installed reviewer node types with readonly project access. The path is an opt-in mode, not assumed present in every workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer models decide the audit verdict based on direct artifact access; deterministic joins merely transport outcomes. Their separation is context/process isolation, not institutional independence from the operator or model supplier. - Claim being audited: builder&#39;s assertion that a branch implements the spec correctly and safely. - Ordinary reporting path: builder&#39;s status, summary and artifact/branch report to the workflow. - Complementary access path: security/UX/code agents in readonly workspaces inspect the actual branch and surrounding code, not just the builder&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: different agent sessions, reviewer roles and readonly source access; all are authored within one user-defined workflow, so a malicious author can weaken the separation. - Who acts on findings: builder corrects the branch when any review requests changes; approver examines the passing result before the user-controlled publication step. - Evidence: factory workflow, PR-review workflow, worktree/delegation behavior. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: source-grounded installed example path, not independently executed S3 evaluation or a universal independent-audit service; routine sequential QA in an arbitrary workflow would not automatically qualify.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally oriented prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external demands or future capabilities could require adaptation, but no shipped owner closes that function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an Ork-run S4. - Decision owner: none demonstrated; workflow authors or maintainers may revise YAML and Ork code outside the run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: captured feedback, event store, local feedback list/note/dismiss, context compiler and user-authored scripts. - Closure path: no shipped feedback-to-accepted-change/lesson path into future current run capability at this revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: collection and grouping of past errors are not themselves a future-oriented model/options/return loop. - Evidence: feedback spec with explicit partial implementation, feedback guide, roadmap. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user may build a separate adaptation workflow; that is not shipped S4 closure in the declared default system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy adjudication path for a workflow organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity or policy tensions can arise, but Ork&#39;s gates and constraints concern routine task/runtime permissions and delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped S5-specific issue, legitimate authority judgment and return-to-operation path. - Decision owner: workflow author/operator defines configuration and controls ordinary approval; those actions are not an evidenced identity-policy process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: YAML settings, tool allowlists, budget caps, askuser, human gates, CLI run controls and local auth. - Closure path: ordinary approvals/stop and permission decisions affect run progression, not an established ultimate-policy matter. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user or agent can edit instruction text or choose publication, but no system-specific S5 right is constructed or closed. - Evidence: workflow format, message and decision controls, factory human gate. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the external human/project may have its own legitimate S5; Ork&#39;s default standard distribution does not supply that governance loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/06adb32ca65514fbe9a0f7c625cdfe64b2c702de/assessments/or/ork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 06adb32ca65514fbe9a0f7c625cdfe64b2c702de\">06adb32 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T17:43:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Synapse</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse/tree/b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\">b913457 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous local production and checking of a specific delegated project deliverable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous requirements, codebase/tool errors, rendered defects and incomplete subtasks encountered during task execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Role model chooses its own next implementation/test tool after observing results. - Decision owner: Role-specific model agent, reached through Synapse-created engine session; human owns separate scope/permission gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenCode adapter, per-role tool rules, session brief, tool transcript, provider retries, bounded worker runner and written-artifact ledger. - Closure path: Model tool call→real engine execution→tool feedback→continuing role turn→worker report/files, then return to Architect. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Multi mode makes genuine model-backed role sessions rather than invoking a development mock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The role&#39;s model has task-sensitive tool/repair choice; Synapse supplies the enclosing execution and evidence channel. - Evidence: OpenCode adapter, runs.js, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high on reachable code path; no independent Windows-provider execution. - Caveats: Actual model quality, installed/packaged engine availability and end-to-end completion were not live-tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Feedback-based regulation of interference or oscillation among operational S1 task agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent edits, shared project plan corruption and worker cross-role overwrite risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No specific agent-owned inter-worker collision detection→mediation→returned adjustment evidenced. - Decision owner: Deterministic harness serialization and folder rules; Architect decides task sequence as S3 commitment, not evidenced as S2 conflict mediation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One-active-worker busy guard per project, roleWriteRules, plan ownership, role folders and assignment ladder. - Closure path: Serial admission and static write rules prevent some conflicts but do not inspect actual inter-unit interference and negotiate mutual change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Transport, isolation and top-down task allocation alone are insufficient for S2 at this recursion. - Evidence: runs.js#L546, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the reviewed first-party orchestration paths. - Caveats: A model could discuss competing tasks in an individual session, but no reusable S2-specific interference closure was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Architect reads the project-wide plan.json task statuses, blocked items, confirmed features and per-worker completion reports grounded in runs.jsonl and actual file/tool records. - Current-control decision scope: Architect alone accepts/rejects task completion, alters the whole plan and selects corrective work and next role commitments within approved project scope; a runner&#39;s retry/backoff merely enforces constraints. - Function: Current project-wide commitment, progress and corrective task regulation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Partial deliverables, faulty worker claims, blocked tasks, failed providers, scope drift and new defects threatening the approved project goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge each reported worker outcome, update the plan, mark done/retry/blocked, reprioritize or delegate the next current task. - Decision owner: Model-driven Architect in Multi mode, constrained by operator-approved features and optional automatic continuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan.json as Architect-only authority, actual runs.jsonl, injected post-worker report, task-role assignment, retry fallback, progress/status rail. - Closure path: Worker run evidence→synthetic report→Architect evaluation/update of plan→next delegate or required operator escalation; Keep building enables continuation, while off returns after each judged task. - Boundary reachability: delegate MCP, run ledger, handleTurn, report and wake are connected in shipped Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the Architect leaves recorded runs and deterministic stops but not discretionary whole-project acceptance, replanning or corrective commitment. - Evidence: runs.js#L2741, plans.js#L1270, delegate MCP. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high; project-control closure is code-visible, not a live success claim. - Caveats: An operator can pause or steer a task, but the documented toggles do not independently establish a distinct parent-governed S3 decision mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Implementing worker&#39;s claim that the shipped build is usable, faithful to approved design and free of target regressions. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder sends its own transcript, modified-file list and completion statement to Architect through runs.jsonl and the synthetic result message. - Complementary access path: Separate QC model role uses the delivered application, renders/screenshots UI, diffs approved mock, tests unusual inputs and writes reproducible findings with IDs independently of worker prose. - Independence boundary: QC has its own configurable model/session and role folder; it cannot edit the build and coders cannot write the QC findings folder under Synapse&#39;s first-party role rules. This is role separation, not tamper-proof institutional independence. - Who acts on findings: Architect reads QC output and delegates the numbered fix to a coder, then assigns verification of that exact repro to QC again, up to the three-round cap. - Function: Independent role-level challenge to implementation claims using real build behavior and a returned quality verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker reports that conceal unusable UI, clipping, misleading states, poor validation or regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: QC chooses and executes adversarial/use-oriented checks, records reproducible findings and re-verifies fixes separately from implementing coders. - Decision owner: Dedicated configured qc model/session, distinct from coder role, with its own findings area and read-only stance toward the build. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QC mandate to render/use the built artifact, screenshot/mock diffs, finding IDs and repros, separate folder permissions, bounded three-round QC/fix/verification plan. - Closure path: QC direct inspection→qcfindings/qc-N.md with evidence→Architect receives report and assigns specific fix task→QC re-runs repros and verifies correction. - Boundary reachability: First-party QC role, delegated real engine session, report loop and role-write restrictions are shipped in Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: QC independently determines and reports actual product defects; the Architect, not QC, retains current-control commitment changes. - Evidence: plans.js#L700, plans.js#L1342, runs.js#L1002. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium: independently tasked source-visible QC path, with no performed cross-provider execution or efficacy audit. - Caveats: The product does not establish strong organizational or cryptographic isolation; different role/model configuration and artifact-based repro constitute the limited claimed independence. If QC is never delegated in a particular plan, the optional path is inactive.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Sensing external/future needs and developing options returned to current project capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unanticipated user needs and future features for the product under construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An Inventor model proposes and ranks extra features; operator chooses scope and Architect links confirmed features to tasks. Whether this amounts to an external sensing→adaptation→S3 loop is unresolved. - Decision owner: Inventor generates options and operator accepts features; no proved future-environment evidence trail connecting the two. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Inventor role mandate, optional local search tools, ideas file, confirmed/rejected features.json and Architect planning instructions. - Closure path: Proposed feature→human selection→confirmed feature/task is explicit; source does not establish that the proposal derived from an observed external prospective distinction rather than imagined additions to a current wish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generating project feature ideas is not, alone, a demonstrated VSM S4 process; the active inventor path prevents a defensible categorical absence claim. - Evidence: plans.js#L660, plans.js#L1287, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: bounded uncertainty. - Caveats: Direct evidence of actual external future-condition observation and its governing choice would resolve the state; neither was reproduced at this source pin.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimate ultimate-policy or system identity dispute resolution at project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts over project identity, ultimate purpose and governing policy rather than current task scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party identity-level issue→authority decision→returned governing policy path shown. - Decision owner: Human operator chooses product features, permission and continuity toggles; those remain operational/requirement decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Feature selection forms, prompt constraints, role-specific permission rules and operator approval. - Closure path: Scope selection reaches project plan, but no recognized ultimate-policy decision channel governs a dispute about the system&#39;s identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choosing which mock to build or whether to continue is not an S5 legitimacy mechanism. - Evidence: plans.js#L1287, automation.js, permissions.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External real-world product governance or developer organizational decisions are out of the assessed standard-distribution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368/assessments/da/darkdvr-synapse.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368\">7211b7a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:22:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HG-ha/Argusai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ArgusAI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HG-ha/Argusai/tree/5f7aa0844ae0341f3563da2a4d171e3fb797d3ac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5f7aa0844ae0341f3563da2a4d171e3fb797d3ac\">5f7aa08 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out security-analysis and general assistant operations through model-selected tools and separate task-performing children. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain code/traffic observations, new user materials, scan errors, tool outputs, environment and evidence changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and sequence domain tools, analyze returned evidence and choose next investigation/validation/report action. - Decision owner: Configured native model-bearing ArgusAI assistant, or connected model-bearing child where given an explicit bounded role; external ACP engines are not credited as ArgusAI&#39;s independent cognitive design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model/tool call loop, domain tool catalog, run/stream state, content archives, confirmation broker and scoped child capability projection. - Closure path: User-defined scope → model/tool call on live workspace → task/evidence output → model inspects and continues/terminates → report or next task state. - Boundary reachability: The native model/tool engine and first-party domain registry are wired into normal GUI/CLI operation, and child tool selection is registered in the shipped assistant. An external ACP model is an alternative supported operating mode, not required for the native witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses substantive task actions; deterministic policy, progress guards and run queues enforce limits rather than owning the choice. - Evidence: assistant loop, tool methods, child execution, README runtime. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for model/tool-path existence; provider execution was not replayed here. - Caveats: A standalone scan engine or GUI table does not own S1 without the configured model session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Independently executing persistent Team Room members, each with distinct objective, tool projection, history, inbox and ongoing work; temporary subagents also exist but do not communicate directly. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Repeated peer status chatter loops, duplicate or out-of-order targeted requests, interruption of busy specialists, concurrent competition for shared member capacity and missed handoff dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: Team member system context requires model discretion to respond only when its role/objective is implicated, to make concrete @role handoffs and otherwise call teamroompass. Durable inboxes deduplicate input references, queue while busy and preserve explicit request ordering; capacity waiters resume as slots release. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A specialist reviews actual targeted peer/leader messages after finishing its current work, chooses to pass, answer with new evidence or issue an exact-role request; that message enters peer inboxes and changes their next work/action choice. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit model-facing anti-loop rule and selective peer-response/handoff control mitigate inter-agent chatter/attention oscillation, while delivery sequencing/dedup solves concrete concurrent-message interference. A generic chat log alone would not establish the agent-owned decision. - Function: Maintain compatible peer coordination without interrupt/reply storms across simultaneous team work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Redundant chat cycles, missed role-specific requests, reentrant live work and shared member-slot interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide when own role must answer an inter-agent request, pass unproductive chatter, or send a bounded dependency/handoff to another specialist. - Decision owner: Model-bearing persistent member agents exercising model-facing Team Room rules; deterministic queue/dedup/capacity checks only supply delivery discipline. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate Team Room log/cursors and pending-input queue, selective @role message route, teamroompass, input reference dedup and member concurrency limits. - Closure path: Peer message/redundant chatter or directed dependency → model member evaluates role fit → pass or targeted correction/handoff → queue records/limits the message → affected member consumes it and adjusts work. - Boundary reachability: Persistent Team Rooms, first-party member prompt and member tools are shipped in the standard GUI/assistant mode; they are not optional third-party skills. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decision whether to escalate/pass/answer is model discretion. Serial/held input alone would not support A, so the positive is restricted to the standing-team model-facing selective coordination path. - Evidence: agent role prompt and member queue, Team Room routing and read cursors, architecture/queue contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; no empirical measured chat-loop reduction is claimed. - Caveats: Temporary spawnteam has no peer messaging; this S2 A is confined to the separately supported persistent-team mode, not all single-agent sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Whole-system current view: Parent assistant can read the complete Todo tree, current task/job progress, team roster, child notices, evidence links and tool outputs; owner GUI sees team state and can edit/steer running membership and assignments. - Current-control decision scope: Model chooses investigation phases, work assignments, task continuation, relevant members, tool batches and response to child results. Owner separately changes standing-team staffing, objective/role assignments and active scope of current work, with next-turn effect. - Function: Coordinate immediate work commitments and evidence production for a whole user objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Specialist readiness, task phase dependencies, uncertain evidence and external tool errors, changing user direction, busy child agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model creates/updates whole-goal Todo plan, delegates and redirects workers; parent owner can edit or pause team members, change scope and direct next assigned work. - Decision owner: Main configured model for autonomous management; installation owner via Team GUI/member update for the parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Todo plan, run locks, live UI, child registry, Team Room and group message stores, confirmation/permission gates. - Closure path: Whole-goal evidence and child notices → model changes priorities/delegation → different agent/tool actions; owner observes team, edits member/assignments or sends new instruction → persisted metadata and injected typed event change subsequent child operation. - Boundary reachability: Both assistant model tools (settodos, spawnteam, agent input) and owner GUI team/member edit controls are first-party installed entry points; they return into running agent sessions rather than isolated dashboards. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Autonomous model retains current resource choices; the user separately holds a legitimate parent current-control role over the same local team. Hard permissions/queues alone do not count as a second owner. - Evidence: model planning/delegation methods, child control/notifications, owner team/member editing. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high within installed modes; no actual provider-backed orchestration run was executed. - Caveats: User task interjections are not credited alone; the distinct parent mode is the current-control owner GUI plus persistent member state returned to operation.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: Distinct independent examination of operational claims via complementary raw reality rather than ordinary task reporting; full closure cannot be established confidently from the reviewed default distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading child report, inaccurate scan/finding, incomplete task claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge a worker/result with raw Case/Artifact/Tool evidence and return a corrective finding to control; whether a governed non-producer audit owner routinely exercises it is unresolved. - Decision owner: Main assistant may inspect material and can instantiate readonly specialists; the built-in reporter role consolidates findings but is not shown to have a mandatory independently sourced veto/repair channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Case/Evidence original references, readonly subagents, result summaries, operator review, tool histories and durable event logs. - Closure path: Child or domain output → main assistant can reread raw evidence and assign further validation → correction to tasks is possible, but a shipped independent complementary inspection owner and repeatable corrective return are not fully proven. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Source supports model review as ordinary operations and a purpose-composable review role, yet cannot distinguish a true independent S3 audit from a task-specific secondary analysis in all supported modes. - Evidence: evidence/case store, main review path, readonly specialist presets/child roles. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium for real audit capability, low for verified S3-specific ownership. - Caveats: webaudit reporter summarizes other roles and the human inspects findings; neither automatically supplies independent unfiltered ground-truth inspection. No presumed external security review is credited.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Adapt future patrol priorities, scheduled investigation or long-lived operational capability from external change; specific closed adaptation ownership remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed attack surface, new file/log events, future alerts and cross-session findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and enact a prospective change to future monitoring/adaptation, distinct from simply completing current steps or running fixed Cron commands. - Decision owner: A model may call first-party cronupsert and alter future work triggers; an installation owner can also configure schedules. However no clearly evidenced shipped model process compares new environment trends, decides a durable revised future capability, and closes that decision back into later operating configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cron tasks with interval/cron/webhook/file-change triggers, persisted jobs/run records, long-term session memory and task/Case references. - Closure path: External event → scheduled session inspects current artifacts and may update Todos/schedule → later trigger executes; the decisive model&#39;s prospective adaptation criterion and actual feedback-based reconfiguration are not resolved at this pin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model-selectable scheduler plus memory offers a credible first-party adaptation candidate, but scheduling fixed repetitions and reusing prior facts alone are not an established S4 owner. - Evidence: model-visible cronupsert catalog, persistent scheduler, scheduler GUI routes, long-term memory. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for future triggers, insufficient for whether they constitute positive S4 adaptation. - Caveats: ? is caused by an identified plausible shipped path, not by a hypothetical future feature; fixed scheduling should not be mistaken for itself owning outside-and-then intelligence.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What a persistent local Team is for, which named specialist roles constitute it, and which role objectives/tool-power boundaries bind members across sessions. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The legitimate self-hosted installation/team owner can create, recharter and edit its standing team and member objectives through first-party GUI/API; no agent-owned constitutional authority to replace that owner&#39;s ultimate charter is established here. - Return-to-operation path: Owner changes standing team purpose or member role/objective/capabilities → persisted team/member record and pending typed metadata update → next member turn receives new role/objective; tool capability projection/confirmation mode enforces limits. - Function: Maintain binding purpose and role identity of the persistent team as a supported parent-governed arrangement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed organizational assignment, durable role constitution and owner-level policy over specialist identity and allowed operating repertoire. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authoritatively change a team/member&#39;s stated continuing purpose, role identity and authorization scope for subsequent work, beyond changing a single tool call. - Decision owner: Installation owner in the supported human-operated GUI/team setup mode; model tools can request team changes but no distinct self-authorized autonomous ultimate-policy closure is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persisted Team metadata, member records, edited role/objective, capability projection and queued metadataupdatepending typed event. - Closure path: Parent decides a standing team/role-purpose revision → updateteammember persists the charter and emits event → member&#39;s future context/inbox incorporates it → subsequent agent work follows updated purpose/policy. - Boundary reachability: Creating and updating persistent teams and members are shipped, first-party GUI/API features, not repository maintainer policy or an external integration. The member worker reads revised metadata on its next safe turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The credited S5 mode is owner-authored standing team constitution, not the assistant approving its own tools or choosing current task steps; S3-level task steering remains separately recorded. - Evidence: persistent team create/update, member metadata revision and next-turn notice, member role/permission API schema. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for the local standing team identity loop. - Caveats: One-off job objectives, approval toggles and permission flags alone are not S5. This claim is limited to the persistent-team role/purpose constitution, not governance of unrelated ArgusAI installations or external security organizations.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/49cc3492e73efe3cbdcbc2bc63dac903deaa2cfd/assessments/ar/argusai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 49cc3492e73efe3cbdcbc2bc63dac903deaa2cfd\">49cc349 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T22:45:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andreseloysv/Tirith\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tirith</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andreseloysv/Tirith/tree/be358146324a8a9c98e6649938205328160c79cf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision be358146324a8a9c98e6649938205328160c79cf\">be35814 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-operated coding and repository task execution in separately launched worktree lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code changes, failing tools/tests, context limits and incomplete user-specified plan items. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select edits, terminal/repository operations and subsequent task actions based on observed tool results and brief constraints. - Decision owner: Configured coding-agent model operating each spawned lane; manager is another model-mediated operational conversation for on-call tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model CLI adapters, lane brief, isolated worktree, agent log reader, plan report, hooks, tool gates, retry and timeout bounds. - Closure path: Open plan item → manager/launcher starts coding-model CLI → model acts in worktree and observes results → reports done/partial/blocked → reviewer/manager feedback may cause a corrected new turn. - Boundary reachability: The shipping lane run/launch command builds supported first-party agent aliases and reads real provider output; plan gate and dispatcher route results into the active project plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Plan reservation, test gates and Git merge logic cannot choose the substantive code edit; only the model agent does. - Evidence: agent runner, launcher, launch contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for wired agent invocation and returned status. - Caveats: Agent binaries/provider accounts are external prerequisites; this review did not execute a paid live coding session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent duplicate concurrent ownership and overlapping write scopes between independent operational lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two plan workers claim the same item, write the same folder or operate on an incomplete prerequisite, causing stale or conflicting source changes. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple separately launched model-agent lanes in individual worktrees, possibly concurrent on separate tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Same task ID can be claimed twice, and explicit write paths can overlap including ancestor/descendant folders. - Attenuating coordination relation: SQLite claim/attempt-token transaction, dependency check, lease/heartbeat, write-path overlap check and reported claim refusal; centralized merge queue serializes integration separately. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Conflicting claims block launch, a missing predecessor remains blocked until actually merged, and released/failed reservations become visible to new launcher/manager decisions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Path-specific atomic exclusivity and stale-attempt fencing address an identified inter-agent resource collision; failure and recovery change which subsequent S1 unit may begin work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine conflict admission and reclaim/release an expired claim after checking worker liveness. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic coordination constructor (SQLite rules and collector recovery), not a model that can change the reservation policy in the operating mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coordination.sqlite3, permanent item IDs, explicit declared writes, lease and attempt token, heartbeat, worker inbox with idempotent messages. - Closure path: Candidate lane → transaction checks existing owners, prerequisites and write overlap → accepted lease or refused conflict → manager/launcher receives available work or blockage and revises later starts. - Boundary reachability: Standard launch acquires reservation before creating worktree/spawning agent; hook/report paths check attempt token; collector recovers dead-owner claims. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Models select task priorities and write declarations but the final conflict verdict, arbitration and lease recovery run deterministically. - Evidence: coordination implementation, coordination contract, merge queue. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for reservation conflicts in declared scopes. - Caveats: Legacy items without declared write paths have no additional path reservation; independent raw shell writes remain bounded by other tool/worktree gates, not this S2 path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the current cross-project plan/worktree portfolio, agent assignments, blocked work, integration and escalation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open work without a worker, stalled/rejected reviews, failed stages, WIP congestion, exhausted agent credits and owner-inbox backlog. - Whole-system current view: Manager brief/queue and Tirith commands expose open plan items, active lanes, status, review/merge queue and recent reports; operator dashboard shows all projects, agents, pipeline results and current owner actions. - Current-control decision scope: Manager chooses split, launch, combine, wait, retry, drop, or ask operator about a current item and handles failed reviews; operator can manually plan-edit, launch/stop agents, switch autopilot and resolve pending current decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Base model manager selects current operational commitments across plan items; human parent can directly alter commitments and stop/relaunch under the installed owner UI. - Decision owner: Base A — persistent on-call manager model. Parent P — human owner acting through Tirith desktop/inbox/plan UI; collector enforces allowed decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Resumable manager session, supervisor queue, autopilot proposals, plan gate, lane launcher, merge WIP limits and auto-repair, user inbox and owner-only actions. - Closure path: Base: collector finds ready/failed work → manager reads its queue/plan → decides agent assignment or follow-up → launched lanes and later results change board/plan and manager&#39;s next turn. Parent: owner sees status/alerts → changes plan, runs/stops lane or toggles autopilot → dispatcher/manager adopt changed current work state. - Boundary reachability: Managerd and dispatch are wired into every collector pass; owner inbox, Tauri commands and plan edits are first-party, directly updating the same live plan/dispatch state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model decides which issue to start, combine or defer based on the live portfolio; deterministic plan/merge gates and load limits enforce but do not choose those priorities. Human current-control is an independent supported mode. - Evidence: manager rules/session, autopilot handoff, inbox contract, mergeq. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped control paths. - Caveats: A manager decision may fail or wait despite the tools. Optional CEO audit influences management but does not replace the base task manager with a guaranteed autonomous global governor.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently check the manager&#39;s account of progress/efficiency against raw collector operational measurements and send corrective directives. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A manager can inaccurately claim progress while no lanes run, reviews are stuck, costs grow or main CI remains red; ordinary manager conversation does not expose all such evidence. - Claim being audited: Whether the manager reliably turns the plan into verified mainline work within the operator&#39;s targets. - Ordinary reporting path: Manager&#39;s own chat, inbox answers, plan/queue decisions and per-lane status reports. - Complementary access path: An opt-in separate fresh CEO model lane gets collector-computed merged/started/review-health counts, scoreboards, age of pending requests and operational CI data; it does not read manager&#39;s private memory/full transcript or accept reports as ground truth. - Independence boundary: CEO has a fresh separate model session, read-only source environment and bounded raw-data snapshot independent of the manager&#39;s claimed status; both run under the same host/provider configuration and the operator controls targets. - Who acts on findings: CEO model judges findings and sends checkable directives to the manager inbox; for budget/code/target proposals it asks the human, who decides. Next CEO runs recheck whether directives improved the measured numbers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Make its own evidence-based operational audit judgment, issue corrective policy-level manager directives and test subsequent efficacy from independent raw counters. - Decision owner: Separate model-driven CEO reviewer in an enabled first-party mode; deterministic claimcheck and stages remain support for worker validation rather than this audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional CEO schedule/force-run entry point, fresh read-only lane, collector measurement snapshot, untrusted-data delimiters, typed result validation, findings/directives records and manager inbox. - Closure path: Measured operational discrepancy → CEO judgment from fresh snapshot → model directive/proposal delivered → manager changes current handling or owner resolves proposal → next CEO pass checks effects and reopens/escalates findings. - Boundary reachability: dispatch.run calls ceo.run on every cycle with [ceo] enabled; ceo.start launches a configured model review lane, and ceo.deliver writes actual manager directives/owner proposals. This opt-in path is shipped, not the planned watchdog. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Snapshot and typed parser do not decide which finding matters or which directive to issue; the distinct CEO model owns that judgment, while worker claimcheck and ordinary tests are deterministic checks. - Evidence: CEO implementation, dispatcher wiring, CEO contract, ordinary claim checker. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party independently wired audit mode; no live CEO model output was reproduced. - Caveats: CEO is off by default. Its code-based snapshot does not prove factual claims outside its observed metrics; some planned CEO watchdog/ratings functions are not shipped.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party loop develops environmental/future options and returns a strategic capability adaptation into active portfolio operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model or tooling changes, future project opportunities and emerging external constraints might change strategy, but regular manager and CEO mostly inspect current/past project performance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Compare prospective external options, select a change to organizational capability and return it to current S3; no such complete chain is established. - Decision owner: Operator/developer chooses provider/model/target configuration; manager decides present plan items and CEO inspects operational KPIs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model fallback chain, Meta summaries, research-capable lanes, plan refresh, CEO targets and autopilot proposal queue. - Closure path: CEO may propose an internal code or budget change and owner may create a task, but there is no required external-environment future-option model or adaptive strategic S4-to-S3 control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Internal throughput monitoring, recovery, scheduling and task planning are not outside-and-then intelligence even if a manager/CEO calls them policy. - Evidence: manager decisions, CEO targets, autopilot. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded absent future function. - Caveats: A user can assign prospective market research work, but that is still a requested S1 task without evidenced strategic S4 return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No separately closed first-party constitutional mission/ultimate-policy dilemma resolution at the project portfolio recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Human-only credentials/payment choices, agent tool safety, target budgets, review and merge policy; these are operational risk/priority bounds. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve a conflict about enduring mission/ethos or ultimate authority between current operations and adaptation, and govern later operations through the decision. - Decision owner: Human owner handles personal/financial decisions and sets target values; CEO can recommend targets and manager execution policy, but not replace owner&#39;s ultimate authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Owner gate/inbox, CEO proposals/approve-decline record, fixed plan/tool/merge hooks, config.toml and planned rules. - Closure path: A CEO recommendation can become an owner approval and plan item for code/budget/targets; this closes scoped operational improvement/authorization, not a demonstrated identity-policy controversy and constitutional return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard rules and a human-final decision are not automatically S5; the cited proposal classes are about task, spend and productivity rather than ultimate identity of the system. - Evidence: owner gate, CEO proposal/decision lifecycle, inbox contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; this is bounded absence, not denial of owner&#39;s real-world governance power. - Caveats: External maintainers and project owners may adopt lasting identity policies beyond the shipped operating workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/75421e2aa581887f73a20a8dc5dc598fe23e4738/assessments/ti/tirith.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 75421e2aa581887f73a20a8dc5dc598fe23e4738\">75421e2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T19:59:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yusa-imit/colony\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">colony</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yusa-imit/colony/tree/b1a7679ac299bf4be2e278cfb25616fac52b3365\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b1a7679ac299bf4be2e278cfb25616fac52b3365\">b1a7679 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Adaptively produce software changes and work artifacts for a claimed task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unspecified implementation steps, code reality, failing checks, rebases and local task obstacles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose implementation, tool sequence, code fixes, testing and completion or release of the current claimed task. - Decision owner: Individual model-bearing worker operating inside Colony&#39;s task protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code CLI process, isolated clone, turn prompt, MCP tools, tests, Git hub, budget gate. - Closure path: Current task/goal → worker reads code → chooses changes → checks observed result → repairs or pushes verified work → records task completion. - Boundary reachability: Colony launches real workers with first-party prompt and task tools on its standard command path; model/service availability is external. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model workers choose real next actions and interpret test feedback; the deterministic board does not write code or select solutions. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L38-L80, worker.rs, hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for operating ownership; no external provider-backed execution in this review. - Caveats: The coding cognition is supplied by an installed external Claude Code worker; Colony owns the protocol/orchestration that directs it, not the model internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Separate code-writing Claude Code worker processes, each owning a different claimed task and isolated clone. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Competing task claims, duplicate work, conflicting changes to shared files and unmet task dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: Workers observe atomic claim feedback and peer messages, change their commitments, reserve dependency-blocked work, negotiate overlap and integrate only after a non-force Git rebase. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A refused claim makes the worker choose another item; a peer mention or rejected push leads to coordination, rebase and retesting before completing its own task. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party protocol connects a named inter-worker collision to its observed refusal or conflict, then instructs the model worker to change its plan and return a non-conflicting commitment. The mechanical lock alone would not suffice. - Function: Attenuate interference between parallel workers changing the same goal/repository and return conflict signals to those workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing claims, duplicate task proposals, overlapping edits, unmet dependencies and stale peer assumptions can destabilize otherwise productive S1 workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Re-select work after a rejected claim, renegotiate task boundaries via peer chat, revise after conflicting Git changes, or reserve/release an interdependent task. - Decision owner: Peer model workers interpret the conflicting board/chat/Git feedback; board and hub provide non-discretionary exclusivity constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Atomic claim/rejection, duplicate proposal filter, dependency reservation, shared chat/notes, mid-turn inbox and non-force-push shared branch. - Closure path: Worker A claims/changes a task → worker B sees refused claim, chat mention or changed hub → B selects another task, coordinates boundaries or rebases → new board/branch state is visible to both. - Boundary reachability: Multiple workers and board tools are the normal Colony runtime, not an external application constructing its own regulator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Locks and rebases alone are automatic S2 support; positive attribution is limited to model-owned choice to replan, coordinate and repair after those feedback signals. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L46-L75, board.rs#L391-L562, inbox.rs, hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high; the code establishes the regulation loop, not conflict-recovery success rates. - Caveats: When only one worker is started, cross-worker S2 is dormant; low-level locking itself is not counted as agent autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Every worker reads the shared board with all open/claimed/done tasks and the common goal, rather than merely its own tool log. - Current-control decision scope: Any worker can propose missing goal work, amend present task dependencies and commitments, reopen failed completion, or close the whole goal after all current tasks finish and configured checks pass. - Function: Distribute current whole-goal task commitments and regulate unresolved work across the shared colony. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Missing goal subtasks, blocked critical paths, stale completed work, all-done-but-goal-incomplete conditions and changed availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: From a whole-board current view, propose a 5–15-task plan, add missing work, pick/reassign one&#39;s commitment, reopen a flawed completed task, and finally declare goal completion. - Decision owner: Distributed model workers; one agent initially takes the planning task but any worker may amend the plan or exercise whole-goal closure. No separate central director exists. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boardview, shared goal, task dependencies/status, board wakeups, one-planning-task bootstrap, goalcomplete eligibility and optional cached verifier. - Closure path: Goal/current board → worker identifies missing or blocked work → proposes/reopens and changes commitments → colleagues see the revised board and act → terminal whole-goal verification/closure. - Boundary reachability: The shipped standard protocol initializes the planning task and gives every worker whole-board tools and goal-completion rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The status checks are deterministic, but agents decide priorities, propose new current work and judge missing completeness; S3 is distributed rather than embodied by a manager process. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L46-L63, prompts.rs#L89-L99, mcp.rs#L188-L204, board.rs#L852-L890. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; no measured claim of effective governance across arbitrary worker counts. - Caveats: No separately privileged S3 agent; the same workers own S1 and distributed S3 choices. Operator-goal changes and budgets remain external.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Claim being audited: A peer&#39;s done task or final shared-branch state meets its acceptance criteria and the colony goal. - Ordinary reporting path: Task owner writes taskcomplete summary with a merged commit; status appears on the board. - Complementary access path: Another worker can inspect the actual shared branch and task details, execute checks, then call taskreopen with a reason; configured verify independently executes an operator-specified test command against the current hub commit. - Independence boundary: Source access and separate peer model process are available but independent reviewer assignment is optional and not enforced; automated verify is a check, not a separate agent judgment. - Who acts on findings: Reopened task becomes claimable and workers are signaled; a failed verify refuses goalcomplete, announces the failure and requires branch repair. - Function: Conditional peer challenge of task completion and reality check of the merged goal state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading done summaries, broken peer changes and unverified whole-goal completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A peer can reject a completion with evidence; optional verifier can block a false goal-complete declaration. - Decision owner: A distinct worker when it undertakes peer inspection; test-command outcome and close-goal gate are deterministic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: taskreopen, source/branch access, task logs, peer notifications, verify commit cache and closure refusal. - Closure path: Owner&#39;s done claim → separate worker inspects actual artifact → reasoned reopen → peer correction/new claim → fresh completion; optional test failure triggers the same operational repair path. - Boundary reachability: Peer-reopen tool and prompt are standard; a different reviewer agent and configured verify command are possible but not required on every completion, warranting C rather than A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The peer can make a substantive source-grounded audit decision; generic testing/board messages are not themselves independent S3 agency. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L89-L99, board.rs#L692-L719, mcp.rs#L269-L285, mcp.rs#L420-L445. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for conditional peer pathway; independent-review coverage not established. - Caveats: taskreopen can be used by the original worker; no source code mandates a distinct auditor. A test failure and its deterministic gate do not prove auditor independence.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No evidenced distinct prospective external scanning and organization-level capability adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current goal implementation, API usage and peer failures, not an evolving future external environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and propagate forward-looking capability/strategy shifts in response to environmental futures; absent. - Decision owner: Operator sets goals, worker count, tools and usage limits; workers can revise within-goal plans only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan task T1, peer notes, repeated sessions, usage-limit throttling and dynamic current tasks. - Closure path: Within-goal task replanning returns to present operations; no prospective option evaluation leading to durable new capability and authorized integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choosing tomorrow&#39;s step of an already fixed software goal is current execution, not S4 environmental adaptation. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L38-L102, supervisor.rs#L1-L14. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for reviewed first-party modes. - Caveats: A user may assign a future-oriented project goal; task subject matter does not establish a distinct harness S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No harness-owned identity/ultimate-policy governance that can legitimately redefine the binding goal and return a constitutional decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User-configured budget, deadlines, permissions and Git restrictions are operational bounds, not identity disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve binding-purpose/policy conflicts with authorized parent return; absent. - Decision owner: Operator supplies goal and budget, Rust supervisor enforces stops, and workers choose within those bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal text, usage caps, task/branch gates, deterministic verifier and human external CLI configuration. - Closure path: Worker goal-complete stops the job and budget stop cancels workers; neither authorizes a change of organizational identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A worker&#39;s finishing declaration is current objective closure (S3), not binding S5 purpose adjudication. - Evidence: supervisor.rs, mcp.rs#L420-L445, prompts.rs#L38-L46. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Operator authorization can govern the colony from outside the reviewed first-party system; no internal legitimate parent role is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/387aebd9a81afa0b50514e12aa1899cc9771776d/assessments/co/colony.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 387aebd9a81afa0b50514e12aa1899cc9771776d\">387aebd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T16:05:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MattGrundy/leadkit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Leadkit</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MattGrundy/leadkit/tree/3efcde576a5f687b0013fad902fe9cff8be11766\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3efcde576a5f687b0013fad902fe9cff8be11766\">3efcde5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement project-scoped coding and verification tasks through agent-selected reading, editing, testing and tool use. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable code requests, test outcomes, repository content and task-specific errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the installed developer/specialist Claude Code Agent decides how to perform its assigned task and reacts to tool observations and reviewer feedback. - Decision owner: active model-driven role Agent in Claude Code Teams, configured by Leadkit&#39;s shipped first-party role definitions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code model/tool host, installed role allowlist, task files, path guard, queue/status verbs and project profile. - Closure path: tools return output to the active agent; it changes/tests code, records evidence and returns its result to task/reviewer queue. - Boundary reachability: standard adoption installs the role definitions and wires hooks; the deployed Host Agent Teams directly consumes these, whereas bare leadkit CLI-only execution is explicitly not claimed to be autonomous. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the role agent chooses substantive code edits and validation steps; CLI/guards enforce boundaries and do not generate the operational choices. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/developer.md#L1-L40; internal/starter/agents/lead.md#L1-L42; internal/starter/starter.go#L1-L85; docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L1-L105. - Basis: explicit / structural within adopted supported mode. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: model execution belongs to external Claude Code; no live paid Claude Code Teams E2E was performed, and disabled Teams yields no autonomous team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: lead-assigned developer and specialist Agents working potentially overlapping git paths and successive reviews, each with its own task/owner identity. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent edits to the same path can silently undo peer work; premature parallel critique can review a SHA invalidated by prior code-review fixes; orphaned task ownership can stall delivery. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead Agent deliberately assigns path ownership, sequences the code-review and critique sessions, returns failures to original owner, and orchestrator Agent resolves stale/idle queue handoffs. Path/queue and gate checks enforce these decisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: reviewer findings change the owner&#39;s next implementation, a changed commit invalidates old approval, and queue/orphan status causes reassignment or escalation rather than duplicate work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: these mechanisms target concrete shared-path collision, stale-SHA review race and lost-ownership hazards and return corrective outcomes to specific work units. - Function: attenuate interference among parallel project Agents so ownership and review outcomes remain consistent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shared-file contention, role drift, stale review race, lost work and duplicate ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead chooses paths and reviewer order; the orchestrator chooses how to handle queue stalls within documented roles; deterministic planner provides constraints and signals. - Decision owner: model-driven Lead/Orchestrator Agents in the shipped enabled-team operating mode, not the Go planner or filesystem alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ownership guard, role lanes, task IDs, deterministic plan, reviewer chain SHA binding and distinct identities. - Closure path: owner assignment enters task store/queue, agents act within scoped paths, review findings return to builder, and next verified SHA advances review/ship stages. - Boundary reachability: these roles and paths are installed through the public adoption workflow and use active Claude Code Teams Agent/SendMessage capabilities, not only project maintainer dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the meaningful assignment and remediation selection belongs to autonomous role Agents; guards deny inconsistent actions but do not choose the route. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/lead.md#L1-L42; internal/starter/agents/orchestrator.md#L1-L28; internal/starter/team-protocol.md#L1-L100; internal/orchestrate/plan.go#L1-L130; internal/gate/chain.go#L65-L140. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for standard deployed role protocol. - Caveats: coordination relies on following first-party role instructions; the Go CLI does not itself launch or monitor a proprietary model session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Whole-system current view: project queue/status/owner/priority/dependency records plus live roster passed in from Agent Teams; account burn telemetry, workstream count, budget reserve and pause-lock status. - Current-control decision scope: choose active team workload and concurrency, defer dispatch when budget is stressed, prioritize critical tasks, requeue orphaned work or grant owner-directed staffing/priority changes. - Function: maintain present project-team commitments, workload and throughput within user resource and authority bounds. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misallocated role capacity, budget burn, stuck tasks and competing current priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party planner exposes S3-specific route/requeue/hold/triage choices, while the installed autonomous actor must assemble a live roster and act on higher-level capacity/priority reports; in the parent mode the human selects the active roster/priority/parallelism and authorizes changes. - Decision owner: C constructor has deterministic CLI and Agent-facing role protocol but lacks a self-contained autonomous S3 governor with all decisive rights; P mode is the legitimate project operator who decides and applies current resource/priority constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: leadkit status, burn/priority planner, usage history, account pause lock, role roster, task queue, apply confirmation, waker/sweep daemons. - Closure path: computed route/hold/escalation is returned to orchestrator or human; accepted priority/roster/budget decisions alter task status or available consumers, and subsequent queue/burn samples expose the result. - Boundary reachability: CLI+roles+hooks/daemons are standard installed interfaces, including explicit owner changes, not private governance; C still needs external Agent Teams and operator adoption, P needs actual human choice. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: consuming a deterministic burn verdict and routing plan does not by itself make the autonomous Lead own a whole-system capacity tradeoff. Parent control is a distinct explicit operating mode rather than a decoration on an approval button. - Evidence: internal/orchestrate/plan.go#L1-L130; internal/orchestrate/apply.go#L1-L80; cmd/leadkit/cmdorchestrate.go#L1-L110; internal/burn/burn.go#L1-L115; internal/burn/throttle.go#L1-L70; docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L20-L105. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium; project-specific actual authorization and agent roster are deployment-specific. - Caveats: automatic pause/resume is deterministic enforcement; the C notation is restricted to S3-specific live-resource decision paths, not arbitrary queue commands. Parent mode presumes a real operator uses reports to revise priority/roster rather than merely installing a static config.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: a code diff at a pinned SHA is correct/safe and actually serves the ticket&#39;s intended goal. - Ordinary reporting path: builder Agent&#39;s completion claim, code changes and test logs. - Complementary access path: separate read-only code-review and critique Agents inspect diff, task and surrounding code under different questions and record own SHA-bound verdicts/evidence. - Independence boundary: distinct installed roles/tools (reviewers lack Edit/Write/SendMessage), order and different-role gate checks; reviewers are not the builder and may not file the other layer&#39;s verdict. - Who acts on findings: original builder/lead receives changes-needed; gate blocks push/PR path until matching independent ready layers, then finalise/human may proceed. - Function: complementary audit of operational output and purpose before a consequential code shipment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-certified implementation errors, wrong goal, stale-SHA approval, and review-role collusion/impersonation accidents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate model-driven reviewer Agents make substantive correctness and goal-fit judgments, each recorded on observed commit evidence. - Decision owner: reviewer Agents own their respective judgments, while Go gate and forge enforcement decide only whether required current, role-separated records exist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer read-only grants, mutation-evidence notes, chain/order, SHA stamps, status/queue, push hooks and optional verified server protection. - Closure path: changes-needed returns work to developer, revised SHA invalidates old approvals, both ready verdicts permit finalise/push within granted authority. - Boundary reachability: reviewer roles and gate are shipped/installable first-party components in the normal team setup, not merely CI tests; the external Claude Code host executes reviewers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic findings belong to independent review Agents; checking SHA/order/identity and command gating is deterministic enforcement. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/code-review-agent.md#L1-L65; internal/starter/agents/critique-agent.md#L1-L85; internal/gate/chain.go#L1-L140; internal/starter/team-protocol.md#L35-L100; cmd/leadkit/cmdgate.go#L1-L95. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party role separation and gate. - Caveats: recorded --by identity may be self-asserted when host does not bind CLAUDEAGENTNAME; local hooks are bypassable without forge protection. Multiple reviewers may share provider/model family; independence is role/evidence separation, not externally certified audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced external-future sensing, adaptation-option judgment and return into current project capability at the assessed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: emerging technology/organizational threats or future opportunities are not a first-party recurrent object of control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S4 actor chooses strategic redesign from external signals and deploys it. - Decision owner: not established; humans may modify roles or open issues; normal Lead coordination operates on present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: feedback-as-issues in overarching-lead protocol, role capture, postmortems, repo governance and backlog. - Closure path: issues preserve potential improvements but no closed strategic adoption loop into team capabilities is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future tasks and capturing lessons is not necessarily outside-and-then adaptation. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/overarching-lead.md#L1-L110; internal/starter/starter.go#L1-L100; README.md#L75-L145. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the user can commission tool upgrades; they remain discretionary external work, not a shipped S4 mode. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether a project team may grant itself standing rights to push, create PRs, merge or modify protected repository policy, versus retaining those fundamental authorization rights at the repository owner. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: direct project/repository owner alone; peer lead and overarching coordinator cannot grant or relay authority in lieu of the owner. - Return-to-operation path: owner-approved authority.&lt;repo&gt; entry in the project profile changes guards and governed PR/push commands; protected-branch rules can be installed and read back, and subsequent operations are permitted/blocked by the returned grant. - Function: reserve ultimate scope of consequential agent action to direct human owner authority and apply it as a durable team constitution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent self-grant, laundering authority through a peer coordinator, unreviewed merges/pushes and inconsistent rights across repositories. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant or deny standing repository mutation/merge authority, and amend protected-branch policy under owner mandate. - Decision owner: legitimate human project/repository owner in the shipped parent-governed setup, not the autonomous Lead or other session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-repo authority profile, adoption stop point, PR/push guard, default-deny allowlists, forge protection verification and doctor. - Closure path: team must request direct owner grant; owner decision is written to profile/forge policy; subsequent hook/CLI operations consume it, permit only granted action and report denials. Denial leaves default-deny intact. - Boundary reachability: authority-grant step and policy guards are part of normal adoption flow and CLI; code explicitly reads named per-repo authority, so the mode is real without relying on GitHub contributors as a metasystem. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents cannot legitimately establish their own standing constitutional authority merely by relaying a request; the owner remains the sole source of grants, while Go enforces the returned decision. - Evidence: docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L20-L105; internal/starter/agents/overarching-lead.md#L1-L110; README.md#L75-L145; docs/design/agent-sandboxing-and-enforcement.md#L1-L95; cmd/leadkit/cmdgate.go#L1-L95. - Basis: explicit / structural parent-governed closure. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party policy path, not for an unseen specific live owner decision. - Caveats: ordinary per-PR human review is not counted as S5; standing authority is the constitutional object. Client-side hooks alone are bypassable; host/forge enforcement and real owner-supplied profile must be configured.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a4ff63c7225d233941c9c33cd63d66584ef61ae4/assessments/le/leadkit.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a4ff63c7225d233941c9c33cd63d66584ef61ae4\">a4ff63c &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
